Guide.content._register("w17", {"id":"w17","order":17,"region":"operate","title":"Governance and Human Oversight","short":"Govern agents","tagline":"Someone must be accountable, and the record must show it.","story":"The agent has acted. A customer, an auditor or a regulator will ask who allowed it, who could have stopped it and how you found out. Here you decide how much human oversight each action deserves, which dated rules and frameworks apply, how a fleet of agents is inventoried and retired, and what you do when something goes wrong.","oneLiner":"Match oversight to action risk and reversibility, then prove it with owners, records and a rehearsed incident process.","youWillLearn":["Choose between approve, two-person review, escalate, sampled review and log-only oversight by flipping reversibility, reach, volume and regulatory class","Date the EU AI Act obligations correctly, including the 2026 Digital Omnibus changes, and say which apply to an assistant","Compare the EU AI Act, NIST AI RMF with its GenAI profile, ISO/IEC 42001 and the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard by what each is and is not","Run an agent lifecycle from inventory to retirement, with Microsoft Agent 365 and Entra Agent ID as the Microsoft stack options","Plan AI incident response with staged containment, the right records and the Article 73 deadlines","Defend a governance programme for a fleet of agents"],"minutes":120,"prereqs":["w07","w12"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w17-fc-001","front":"Name the oversight patterns and the trigger for each.","back":"Approve before action (irreversible or externally visible), two-person review (highest risk), escalate on a trigger (low confidence or policy hit), sampled review with undo (high volume, reversible), log and alert only (low risk).","tags":["oversight"]},{"id":"w17-fc-002","front":"What does EU AI Act Article 14 expect of human oversight for high-risk systems?","back":"Measures proportionate to risk, autonomy and context. People can understand limits, stay aware of automation bias, interpret output, decide not to use or override it, and interrupt with a stop control.","tags":["eu-ai-act","oversight"]},{"id":"w17-fc-003","front":"When do Annex III high-risk rules apply after the Digital Omnibus?","back":"2027-12-02. Annex I product-embedded systems follow on 2028-08-02. Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 was published 2026-07-24 and entered into force 2026-07-27. Posts that say 2026-08-02 are out of date.","tags":["eu-ai-act","dates"]},{"id":"w17-fc-004","front":"What began on 2026-08-02 under the EU AI Act?","back":"Article 50 transparency rules apply, and enforcement starts for GPAI obligations, prohibitions, transparency and AI literacy. Systems on the market before that date have until 2026-12-02 for Article 50(2) marking.","tags":["eu-ai-act","dates"]},{"id":"w17-fc-005","front":"EU AI Act maximum fines under Article 99?","back":"Prohibited practices: EUR 35M or 7% of worldwide turnover. Other operator obligations: EUR 15M or 3%. Incorrect information: EUR 7.5M or 1%. Undertakings pay the higher figure, SMEs the lower (small mid-caps too on the 3% and 1% tiers).","tags":["eu-ai-act","fines"]},{"id":"w17-fc-006","front":"Serious incident reporting deadlines for high-risk systems (Article 73)?","back":"No later than 15 days after becoming aware, 2 days for widespread infringements or Article 3(49)(b) incidents, and 10 days after a death. An initial incomplete report is allowed.","tags":["eu-ai-act","incident"]},{"id":"w17-fc-007","front":"What is the NIST AI RMF, and how is the GenAI profile related?","back":"A voluntary framework (1.0 released 2023-01-26, under revision) with four functions: Govern, Map, Measure, Manage. NIST AI 600-1 (2024-07-26) is the GenAI profile with twelve risks.","tags":["nist"]},{"id":"w17-fc-008","front":"What does ISO/IEC 42001 certify?","back":"An organization's AI management system (AIMS), run as Plan-Do-Check-Act. Published December 2023 and certifiable. It does not certify one application's behaviour.","tags":["iso-42001"]},{"id":"w17-fc-009","front":"What is in the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard v2?","back":"A June 2022 external release with six goal groups: accountability, transparency, fairness, reliability and safety, privacy and security, inclusiveness. It includes goal A5 human oversight and control and a Sensitive Uses review at least annually.","tags":["microsoft-rai"]},{"id":"w17-fc-010","front":"List the agent governance lifecycle stages.","back":"Inventory, identity, permissions, review, retirement. An agent that is not in the inventory has no sponsor, no review date and no retirement path.","tags":["lifecycle"]},{"id":"w17-fc-011","front":"What is Microsoft Agent 365?","back":"A control plane to observe, govern and secure agents through a registry, Entra, Purview and Defender. Generally available 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, licensed per user.","tags":["agent-365"]},{"id":"w17-fc-012","front":"Entra Agent ID: sponsor versus owner?","back":"Sponsors are business representatives, at least one required, accountable for purpose and lifecycle. They can disable or soft-delete but not edit auth. Owners are optional technical admins who manage credentials and can re-enable or hard-delete.","tags":["entra-agent-id"]},{"id":"w17-fc-013","front":"How are agents governed when they cannot do MFA?","back":"With Conditional Access, evaluated when a token is issued or refreshed, and ID Governance access packages for agent identities. Licensing requirements apply.","tags":["entra-agent-id","identity"]},{"id":"w17-fc-014","front":"How does Microsoft Agent Framework gate a tool call on a human?","back":"Python: `@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")` and check `result.user_input_requests`. .NET: wrap the function in `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction`. Approvals bind to a pending request in the same session by default.","tags":["approval","agent-framework"]},{"id":"w17-fc-015","front":"Microsoft's three stages of AI incident remediation?","back":"Contain in the first hour, expand and strengthen mitigations in the first 24 hours, fix at source over days or weeks. Add watch periods after each stage because behaviour is non-deterministic.","tags":["incident"]},{"id":"w17-fc-016","front":"Why is AI incident severity hard to rank?","back":"Severity depends on domain, affected people and content, not record count. Root cause can span data, fine-tuning, retrieval and context, and standard telemetry misses output anomalies.","tags":["incident"]},{"id":"w17-fc-017","front":"What does Article 50(1) require of AI systems that interact with people?","back":"Providers must design them so people are told they are interacting with an AI system, unless that is obvious from context. The information must be clear at the latest at the first interaction.","tags":["eu-ai-act","transparency"]},{"id":"w17-fc-018","front":"What must high-risk systems record under Article 12?","back":"Automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's lifetime, enough for risk identification, post-market monitoring and monitoring of operation.","tags":["eu-ai-act","logging"]},{"id":"w17-t01-fc1","front":"In, on and over the loop?","back":"In: a person approves each decision. On: the system acts and a person can interrupt. Over: people set policy and audit samples afterwards. Definitions vary by source.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t01"},{"id":"w17-t01-fc2","front":"What does Article 14 require of oversight people?","back":"Understand limits, stay aware of automation bias, interpret output, override and interrupt with a stop control. Two competent people for Annex III point 1(a).","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t01"},{"id":"w17-t01-fc3","front":"What is the Agents Rule of Two?","back":"In one session, avoid combining untrusted input, sensitive access and external effects. If all three are needed, require human approval.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t01"},{"id":"w17-t02-fc1","front":"How do you declare approval in Agent Framework?","back":"Python: `@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")` with `user_input_requests` on the result. .NET: wrap in `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction`, response carries `ToolApprovalRequestContent`.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t02"},{"id":"w17-t02-fc2","front":"Why tier by action class, not tool name?","back":"Risk depends on effect and arguments. Another tool or a larger amount can reach the same harm past a per-tool flag.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t02"},{"id":"w17-t02-fc3","front":"What should a timeout do?","back":"Fail closed: no decision means no action. Expire visibly and give urgent cases an on-call path.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t02"},{"id":"w17-t03-fc1","front":"What do Article 12 and Article 26(6) require?","back":"Art 12: automatic event recording over the lifetime of high-risk systems. Art 26(6): deployers keep logs under their control at least six months unless other law differs.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t03"},{"id":"w17-t03-fc2","front":"Who is accountable in the Entra Agent ID model?","back":"The sponsor is accountable for purpose and lifecycle, with at least one per agent identity. The owner administers configuration and credentials.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t03"},{"id":"w17-t03-fc3","front":"Default content policy for audit logs?","back":"Ids and decisions by default. Content only for a bounded window with its own access control.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t03"},{"id":"w17-t04-fc1","front":"When do Annex III high-risk rules apply after the Omnibus?","back":"2027-12-02. Annex I product-embedded systems follow on 2028-08-02. Article 50 and GPAI enforcement began 2026-08-02.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t04"},{"id":"w17-t04-fc2","front":"Article 99 maximum fines?","back":"EUR 35M or 7% for prohibited practices, EUR 15M or 3% for other operator duties, EUR 7.5M or 1% for incorrect information. Higher figure for undertakings, lower for SMEs (and small mid-caps on the 3% and 1% tiers).","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t04"},{"id":"w17-t04-fc3","front":"Is the GPAI Code of Practice law?","back":"No. Published 2025-07-10, it is voluntary, with Transparency, Copyright and Safety and Security chapters.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t04"},{"id":"w17-t05-fc1","front":"What are the four AI RMF functions?","back":"Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. Govern is cross-cutting. AI RMF 1.0 was released 2023-01-26 and is voluntary and under revision.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t05"},{"id":"w17-t05-fc2","front":"What is NIST AI 600-1?","back":"The Generative AI Profile, released 2024-07-26, listing twelve GenAI risks, with prompt injection and data poisoning under information security.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t05"},{"id":"w17-t05-fc3","front":"Can you be NIST certified?","back":"No. It is voluntary guidance. Say aligned and show evidence for each mapped control.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t05"},{"id":"w17-t06-fc1","front":"What is ISO/IEC 42001?","back":"A certifiable AI management system standard (AIMS), published December 2023, run as Plan-Do-Check-Act. It governs organizational practice, not one application's behaviour.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t06"},{"id":"w17-t06-fc2","front":"What are the six goal groups of Microsoft's RAI Standard v2?","back":"Accountability, Transparency, Fairness, Reliability and Safety, Privacy and Security, Inclusiveness. Includes A5 human oversight and an annual Sensitive Uses review.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t06"},{"id":"w17-t06-fc3","front":"Law, guidance, standard, rulebook?","back":"EU AI Act is law. NIST is voluntary guidance. ISO 42001 is a certifiable standard. The RAI Standard is an internal rulebook.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t06"},{"id":"w17-t07-fc1","front":"Five stages of agent governance?","back":"Inventory, identity, permissions, review, retirement. An unlisted agent is an ungoverned agent.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t07"},{"id":"w17-t07-fc2","front":"Sponsor versus owner in Entra Agent ID?","back":"Sponsor: required business representative accountable for purpose and lifecycle. Owner: optional technical administrator who also manages credentials.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t07"},{"id":"w17-t07-fc3","front":"When was Agent 365 generally available?","back":"2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, per user, with Microsoft E5 as best fit. Shadow-agent discovery was announced as preview then.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t07"},{"id":"w17-t08-fc1","front":"What does a model card do?","back":"A short document accompanying a model that reports performance across conditions (Mitchell et al.). Datasheets for datasets do the same for data (Gebru et al.).","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t08"},{"id":"w17-t08-fc2","front":"Where must Article 50 information appear?","back":"Clear and distinguishable, at the latest at the first interaction or exposure, and accessible. A terms-of-service clause is not enough.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t08"},{"id":"w17-t08-fc3","front":"What do GPAI providers document under Article 53?","back":"Technical documentation (Annex XI), downstream provider information (Annex XII), a copyright policy and a public training-content summary. Open-source models are exempt from the first two unless systemic-risk.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t08"},{"id":"w17-t09-fc1","front":"Why do AI incidents differ from classic security incidents?","back":"Taxonomy gaps, context-dependent severity, ambiguous root cause and telemetry gaps. Outputs are probabilistic and one gap can cause thousands of harms.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t09"},{"id":"w17-t09-fc2","front":"What are the three remediation stages?","back":"Contain in the first hour, expand in the first 24 hours, fix at source over days or weeks, with a watch period after each.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t09"},{"id":"w17-t09-fc3","front":"What are the Article 73 reporting limits?","back":"15 days after awareness, 2 days for widespread infringement or Article 3(49)(b), 10 days after a death. An incomplete initial report is allowed.","tags":[],"topic":"w17-t09"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Timeline for the implementation of the EU AI Act","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act","type":"doc","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"The authoritative dated milestones after the Omnibus.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r2","title":"AI Omnibus enters into force","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-omnibus-enters-force","type":"doc","publisher":"European Commission","note":"What the 2026 amendment changed, including the new high-risk dates.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r3","title":"EU AI Omnibus enters into force, amending the AI Act","url":"https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/eu-ai-omnibus-enters-force-amending-ai-act","type":"blog","publisher":"White & Case","note":"Source for the signature, publication and in-force dates. Secondary source, returns 403 to scripted requests.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r4","title":"EU AI Act Article 99: penalties","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-99","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"The three fine tiers and the higher-or-lower rule for undertakings and SMEs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"EU AI Act Article 14: human oversight","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-14","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"What oversight must let a person do, including automation bias awareness.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r6","title":"EU AI Act Article 50: transparency obligations","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-50","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Disclosure to people, machine-readable marking and deep fake disclosure.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r7","title":"EU AI Act Article 73: reporting of serious incidents","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-73","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"The 15, 2 and 10 day reporting deadlines and the initial-report rule.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"EU AI Act Article 12: record-keeping","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-12","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Automatic event logging for high-risk systems.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r9","title":"General-Purpose AI Code of Practice","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai","type":"doc","publisher":"European Commission","note":"The voluntary code published 2025-07-10 with three chapters.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"spec","publisher":"NIST","note":"Govern, Map, Measure, Manage, and the note that 1.0 is under revision.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"NIST AI 600-1: Generative AI Profile","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","type":"spec","publisher":"NIST","note":"The twelve GenAI risks and suggested actions, released 2024-07-26.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"ISO/IEC 42001:2023 Artificial intelligence management system","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001","type":"spec","publisher":"ISO","note":"The certifiable AI management system standard. Returns 403 to scripted requests but loads in a browser.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"Microsoft Responsible AI Standard, v2 General Requirements","url":"https://blogs.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/prod/sites/5/2022/06/Microsoft-Responsible-AI-Standard-v2-General-Requirements-3.pdf","type":"spec","publisher":"Microsoft","note":"Goal-based governance including human oversight and control, published June 2022.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Microsoft Purview data security and compliance protections for generative AI apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/ai-microsoft-purview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"DSPM for AI, labels and the EXTRACT usage right.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"AI threat protection in Microsoft Defender for Cloud","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/defender-for-cloud/ai-threat-protection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"GA alerts for leakage, poisoning and jailbreak, with scope limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Using function tools with human in the loop approvals (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/tools/tool-approval","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The built-in approval hook and the approval binding rule.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Microsoft Agent 365 overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-agent-365/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Observe, govern, secure, and the 2026-05-01 GA for the Commercial segment.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Owners, sponsors and managers in Microsoft Entra Agent ID","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/agent-id/agent-owners-sponsors-managers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Business accountability versus technical administration for agents. The linked Graph resources are in the beta API.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"Conditional Access for agent identities","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/agent-id","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Why agents are governed with Conditional Access and what licensing applies.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r20","title":"Agent registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/admin/manage/agent-registry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The four registry types and where the inventory lives.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Incident response for AI systems","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/zero-trust/sfi/incident-response-ai-systems","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Why AI incidents differ and the three-stage remediation pattern, page dated 2026-04-12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications: the benchmark for agentic security","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-the-benchmark-for-agentic-security-in-the-age-of-autonomous-ai/","type":"blog","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"ASI03 Identity and Privilege Abuse and ASI09 Human-Agent Trust Exploitation bear on oversight.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Agents Rule of Two: A Practical Approach to AI Agent Security","url":"https://ai.meta.com/blog/practical-ai-agent-security/","type":"blog","publisher":"Meta","note":"When all three risky properties are needed, require human approval. Dated 2025-10-31.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Microsoft Agent 365 is generally available","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/01/microsoft-agent-365-now-generally-available-expands-capabilities-and-integrations/","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft Security Blog","note":"The 2026-05-01 announcement with GA and preview capabilities. Returns 403 to scripted requests.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r25","title":"Study: Govern AI usage with Microsoft Purview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/purview-ai-govern-usage/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Practical governance controls for AI apps.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Study: Implement a responsible generative AI solution in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/responsible-ai-studio/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Guardrails and responsible AI practice on the Microsoft stack.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r27","title":"Agent identity in Microsoft Agent 365 (developer guide)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-agent-365/developer/identity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Blueprints, agent identities, agent users, sponsors and the three execution modes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"EU AI Act Article 26: obligations of deployers of high-risk AI systems","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-26","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Human oversight assigned to competent people, monitoring, inform the provider, logs kept at least six months.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"EU AI Act Article 4: AI literacy","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-4","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Providers and deployers support AI literacy of staff; text marked as amended by the Omnibus.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"EU AI Act Article 11: technical documentation","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-11","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Annex IV documentation before market placement, kept up to date, simplified form for SMEs and small mid-caps.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"EU AI Act Article 53: obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-53","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Annex XI and XII documentation, copyright policy, training-content summary and the open-source exemption.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"EU AI Act Article 6: classification rules for high-risk AI systems","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-6","type":"spec","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Annex I and Annex III routes, the Article 6(3) derogation and the profiling rule.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Model Cards for Model Reporting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Mitchell et al.)","note":"The original proposal for short documents that accompany a trained model.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Datasheets for Datasets","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Gebru et al.)","note":"The documentation counterpart for datasets.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Transparency Note for Azure OpenAI in Microsoft Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/responsible-ai/openai/transparency-note","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What a Transparency Note is and how system owners use it.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr9","title":"NIST AI RMF Playbook","url":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/","type":"doc","publisher":"NIST","note":"Suggested actions per sub-category, voluntary, to be updated after the AI RMF revision.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"LLM08:2025 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm082025-vector-and-embedding-weaknesses/","type":"doc","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Recommends permission-aware stores and immutable retrieval logs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Human in the loop","def":"A person approves or decides before the system acts. The strongest brake and the most friction."},{"term":"Human on the loop","def":"A person monitors and can intervene, but the system acts without waiting. Sampled review and alerting sit here."},{"term":"Automation bias","def":"The tendency to over-trust system output. Article 14 expects overseers to stay aware of it."},{"term":"Rubber-stamping","def":"Approvals given without real review because volume, time pressure or a bare yes or no screen makes careful review impossible."},{"term":"Reversibility","def":"How cheaply an action can be undone. The first dial for choosing the oversight level."},{"term":"Provider and deployer","def":"EU AI Act roles. A provider develops or places an AI system on the market. A deployer uses it under its own authority. Duties differ by role."},{"term":"High-risk system","def":"An AI system in an Annex III use area or an Annex I product. Its core duties apply from 2027-12-02 and 2028-08-02 after the Digital Omnibus."},{"term":"AIMS","def":"AI management system, the organization-wide system that ISO/IEC 42001 specifies and certifies."},{"term":"Agent sponsor","def":"The business representative accountable for an agent's purpose and lifecycle in Entra Agent ID. At least one is required."},{"term":"Agent registry","def":"The inventory of agents with owners, identities and status. In Agent 365 it lives in the Microsoft 365 admin center."},{"term":"Sensitive Uses review","def":"A Microsoft Responsible AI Standard review for uses with a higher risk of harm, repeated at least annually."},{"term":"Serious incident","def":"An incident of a high-risk system that the EU AI Act requires its provider to report to market surveillance authorities within the Article 73 deadlines of 15, 2 or 10 days."},{"term":"Annex III","def":"The EU AI Act list of use areas that make a system high-risk, such as certain decisions about people. Its rules apply from 2027-12-02 after the 2026 Omnibus."},{"term":"GPAI model","def":"A general-purpose AI model under the EU AI Act. Providers owe documentation, a copyright policy and a training-content summary, with extra duties for systemic-risk models."},{"term":"Digital Omnibus on AI","def":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 2026-07-27, which moved the high-risk dates and made other changes to the AI Act."},{"term":"Model card","def":"A short document accompanying a trained model that reports how it performs across conditions and states its intended use and limits."},{"term":"System card","def":"Your own versioned document for a deployed AI system: purpose, exclusions, data and permissions, model and prompt versions, eval results, limits, oversight design and contacts."},{"term":"Transparency Note","def":"Microsoft's document that explains how a technology works, the choices system owners can make and why to consider the whole system."},{"term":"Fail closed","def":"A design where a missing decision, timeout or error blocks the action instead of allowing it."},{"term":"Staged remediation","def":"Responding to an AI incident in three stages: contain in the first hour, expand in the first 24 hours and fix at source over days or weeks, with watch periods."},{"term":"Kill switch","def":"A tested control that disables an agent, tool or identity quickly during an incident, for example by suspending the agent identity."},{"term":"AI literacy","def":"Measures that providers and deployers take so staff and others who operate AI systems understand them, required by Article 4 of the EU AI Act."}],"levels":[{"id":"w17-l1","order":1,"title":"Who holds the brake?","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"An agent is about to act. Decide whether a person approves first, is pulled in on a trigger, reviews a sample afterwards or only sees a log, while reversibility, reach, volume and regulatory class are still open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four brakes and their price","level":2,"id":"four-brakes-and-their-price"},{"t":"p","md":"Oversight is a brake with a price. **Approve before action** stops harm before it lands but adds friction and invites rubber-stamping. **Escalate on a trigger** pulls a person in only for hard cases. **Sampled review with undo** accepts that some mistakes land and relies on cheap reversal. **Log and alert only** gives accountability after the fact, not a brake.\n\nLaw and design heuristics agree on the shape. For high-risk systems Article 14 expects oversight proportionate to risk, autonomy and context: people can understand the system's limits, stay aware of automation bias, interpret output, decide not to use or override it, and interrupt it with a stop control. For biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a), two competent people must verify separately before action [[r5]]. Meta's Rule of Two says that when a session needs untrusted input, sensitive access and the ability to change state or communicate externally, require human approval [[r23]]. A reviewer who sees a tool name and a yes button is not exercising oversight, so design the screen to show the evidence and the arguments."},{"t":"compare","title":"Oversight patterns","intro":"Each pattern names where it wins, where it loses and what flips the choice.","options":[{"name":"Approve before action","summary":"A person approves each action, or each action above a threshold, before it runs. A two-person variant adds a second independent reviewer.","pros":["Stops harm before it lands","A named approver gives clear accountability"],"cons":["Friction and queues","Rubber-stamping at volume"],"bestWhen":"Irreversible or externally visible actions at modest volume.","avoidWhen":"Thousands of reversible actions a day.","meta":[{"k":"Friction","v":"high"},{"k":"Harm stopped","v":"before it lands"}]},{"name":"Escalate on a trigger","summary":"The agent acts alone until a rule fires: low confidence, a large amount, sensitive data or a policy hit.","pros":["Humans see only the hard cases","Scales with volume"],"cons":["Only as good as the trigger","A narrow trigger hides costly misses"],"bestWhen":"High volume with a risky tail you can describe.","avoidWhen":"Irreversible actions where one missed trigger is unacceptable.","meta":[{"k":"Friction","v":"medium"},{"k":"Depends on","v":"trigger quality"}]},{"name":"Sampled review with undo","summary":"Actions run, a sample is reviewed afterwards and mistakes are reversed.","pros":["Almost no friction","Measures the real error rate"],"cons":["Damage lands before review","Needs a real undo"],"bestWhen":"High volume, reversible actions with low reach.","avoidWhen":"Irreversible or externally visible actions.","meta":[{"k":"Friction","v":"low"},{"k":"Harm stopped","v":"after the fact"}]},{"name":"Log and alert only","summary":"Record every action and alert on anomalies, with no person in the path.","pros":["No friction","A complete audit trail"],"cons":["No brake before harm","Gives no means to intervene, which Article 14 expects for high-risk systems"],"bestWhen":"Low-risk, reversible, internal actions.","avoidWhen":"Anything with external reach or a regulated use.","meta":[{"k":"Friction","v":"none"},{"k":"Harm stopped","v":"none"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Set the oversight level","level":2,"id":"set-the-oversight-level"},{"t":"dials","id":"w17-l1-brake","title":"How much human oversight does this action need?","prompt":"Set how reversible the action is, how far it reaches, how many a day and whether the use is high-risk under the AI Act. The best brake changes.","dials":[{"id":"rev","label":"Reversibility","values":[{"id":"easy","label":"Easy to undo"},{"id":"costly","label":"Costly to undo"},{"id":"irrev","label":"Irreversible"}]},{"id":"reach","label":"Reach","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One user or record"},{"id":"many","label":"Many people or externally visible"}]},{"id":"volume","label":"Volume","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Dozens a day"},{"id":"high","label":"Thousands a day"}]},{"id":"regime","label":"Regulatory class","values":[{"id":"unreg","label":"Not regulated as high-risk"},{"id":"high","label":"High-risk use under the AI Act"}]}],"options":[{"id":"approve","name":"Approve before action","summary":"One person approves each action."},{"id":"dual","name":"Two-person review","summary":"Two independent reviewers approve."},{"id":"escalate","name":"Escalate on a trigger","summary":"A person joins when a rule fires."},{"id":"sample","name":"Sampled review with undo","summary":"Review a sample after the fact."},{"id":"audit","name":"Log and alert only","summary":"Record everything, alert on anomalies."}],"scores":{"approve":{"rev":{"easy":{"s":-1,"why":"Friction for an action you can undo."},"costly":{"s":1,"why":"Worth a person's time."},"irrev":{"s":2,"why":"A human gate before the point of no return."}},"reach":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Visible mistakes justify the pause."}},"volume":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"A person can keep up."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"The queue grows and approvals become rubber stamps."}},"regime":{"unreg":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Article 14 expects the means to override and stop."}}},"dual":{"rev":{"easy":{"s":-2,"why":"Heavy for reversible actions."},"costly":{"s":0,"why":"Reserve it for the costliest cases."},"irrev":{"s":2,"why":"Two independent checks before the point of no return."}},"reach":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"Overkill for one record."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Many affected people justify two reviewers."}},"volume":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Affordable only at low volume."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Unaffordable at volume."}},"regime":{"unreg":{"s":-1,"why":"Overkill."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Some high-risk uses require two separate verifications (Article 14(5))."}}},"escalate":{"rev":{"easy":{"s":1,"why":"Pull a person in only when unsure."},"costly":{"s":1,"why":"Triggers catch the risky slice."},"irrev":{"s":0,"why":"A confidence trigger alone is not a gate for irreversible actions."}},"reach":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Fine for contained mistakes."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"A missed trigger is visible to many."}},"volume":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Humans see only the hard cases."}},"regime":{"unreg":{"s":1,"why":"Reasonable."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Needs documented triggers and a real override path."}}},"sample":{"rev":{"easy":{"s":1,"why":"Undo makes after-the-fact review safe."},"costly":{"s":-1,"why":"Damage lands before review."},"irrev":{"s":-2,"why":"Review arrives after the point of no return."}},"reach":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"A mistake affects one person."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"A bad batch spreads before anyone samples."}},"volume":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Few items to sample."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"The only way to cover thousands of actions."}},"regime":{"unreg":{"s":1,"why":"Acceptable."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Not sufficient alone for high-risk oversight."}}},"audit":{"rev":{"easy":{"s":2,"why":"Cheap when mistakes are fixable."},"costly":{"s":-1,"why":"A log does not undo harm."},"irrev":{"s":-2,"why":"No brake before harm."}},"reach":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Contained mistakes are tolerable."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Nobody stops a wide mistake."}},"volume":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Scales to any volume."}},"regime":{"unreg":{"s":1,"why":"Acceptable for low-risk uses."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Logs are required but are not oversight."}}}},"takeaway":"Reversibility and reach pick the family: approval for irreversible, sampling for reversible. Volume and regulatory class then adjust it. Say which dial moved you."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"Approval rate sits near 100% and decisions take two seconds.","cause":"Volume or a bare yes or no screen has turned approval into rubber-stamping, the automation bias Article 14 warns about.","fix":"Show arguments and evidence, seed known-bad items and track the catch rate, and move low-risk actions to sampled review."},{"symptom":"A risky tool runs after a client message claims it was approved.","cause":"An unbound or replayed approval. By default the framework authorizes a call only when the response matches a pending request in the same session.","fix":"Keep approval binding on. If you disable it, bind approvals to request id, user and session in your own gate [[r16]]."},{"symptom":"The gated tool is never called, yet the same effect happens.","cause":"The gate sits on one tool while a second tool, an MCP server or an API reaches the same effect.","fix":"Gate by action class at a gateway or policy layer and test every path that can produce the effect."},{"symptom":"Escalations are rare and a costly mistake slips through.","cause":"A confidence trigger that is too narrow or poorly calibrated.","fix":"Add risk triggers (amount, external recipient, data class), audit what was not escalated and tune with seeded cases."}]},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Python","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Gate a tool and read the approval request","code":"from agent_framework import tool\n\n@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Refund an order.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\nresult = await agent.run(\"Refund order 1182\", session=session)\nfor req in result.user_input_requests:\n    if req.function_call is not None:\n        show_reviewer(req.function_call.name, req.function_call.arguments)","caption":"Reply with req.to_function_approval_response(True or False) in a new run on the same session."}]},{"label":".NET","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"csharp","title":"Wrap the function so it needs approval","code":"AIFunction refund = AIFunctionFactory.Create(IssueRefund);\nAIFunction gated = new ApprovalRequiredAIFunction(refund);\n// Pass gated in the agent's tools. The response then carries\n// ToolApprovalRequestContent instead of a final answer."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"An agent issues refunds. Refunds under 20 units are easy to reverse and run in the thousands each day. Refunds above 5,000 units are hard to claw back and run a few times a day. Which oversight design fits?","options":["Approve every refund","Sampled review with undo for small refunds and approval for large ones","Log and alert only for every refund","Two-person review for every refund"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reversibility and volume point in opposite directions across the two tiers, so you tier the brake. Approving thousands of small refunds creates a queue and rubber-stamping, logging alone gives no brake before a 5,000 unit loss, and two-person review for everything is unaffordable at volume.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"},{"id":"w17-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"What does this setting risk unless your application enforces an equivalent binding?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"client.function_invocation_configuration[\"disable_approval_response_binding\"] = True"},"options":["Approvals expire after one minute","Tool calls stop being gated at all","Approvals are shared across users in the same tenant","A fabricated or replayed response can authorize a privileged tool call"],"answer":3,"explain":"By default a local approval authorizes a call only when it matches a pending request in the same session. Disabling the binding restores unbound behaviour, so a forged or replayed response can approve a privileged call. Gating itself stays on, there is no one-minute expiry in that setting and it does not share approvals across tenants.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w17-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every control that reduces automation bias on an approval screen (design reasoning, consistent with Article 14).","options":["Show the arguments, retrieved evidence and a diff of the change","Require a written reason for approvals above a threshold","Seed known-bad items and track the reviewer catch rate","Preselect the approve button to save time","Track decision time and approval rate per reviewer"],"answers":[0,1,2,4],"explain":"Evidence, justification, seeded errors and decision metrics all make careless approval visible or harder. A preselected approve button does the opposite and encourages the automation bias Article 14 asks overseers to resist.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r5","r16","r23"],"takeaways":["Reversibility and reach choose the family of brake. Volume and regulatory class adjust it.","Approval without evidence is not oversight. Show arguments, seed bad items and measure the catch rate.","Bind approvals to the pending request, the user and the session so they cannot be replayed.","Gate by action class, not by one tool, or the effect leaks through another path.","When all three Rule of Two properties are needed, a human approves."]},{"id":"w17-l2","order":2,"title":"Read the calendar, not the blog post","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A customer asks which AI rules apply to your assistant and from when. Date every obligation, name your role as provider or deployer, and check that the date on your slide is not one that moved on 2026-07-27.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Dates that decide the roadmap","level":2,"id":"dates-that-decide-the-roadmap"},{"t":"table","caption":"EU AI Act milestones after the Digital Omnibus, checked 2026-10-13 [[r1]].","head":["Date","What applies","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2024-08-01","The AI Act enters into force","Start of the staged calendar"],["2025-02-02","Prohibitions and AI literacy obligations","Banned practices are already off the table"],["2025-08-02","General-purpose AI (GPAI) obligations","Model providers, and teams that fine-tune or rebrand models, check their role"],["2026-08-02","Article 50 transparency rules apply. Enforcement starts for GPAI, prohibitions, transparency and AI literacy","Chatbots must tell people they talk to an AI system"],["2026-12-02","New prohibitions, and the end of the Article 50(2) marking transition for systems already on the market","Machine-readable marking of synthetic content"],["2027-12-02","Annex III high-risk rules, moved from 2026-08-02 by the Digital Omnibus","Articles 12 and 14 logging and oversight for those uses"],["2028-08-02","Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules","Safety components of regulated products"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"p","md":"The Digital Omnibus on AI is Regulation (EU) 2026/1744. It was signed on 2026-07-08, published in the Official Journal on 2026-07-24 and entered into force on 2026-07-27 [[r3]]. It moves Annex III high-risk obligations to 2027-12-02 and Annex I to 2028-08-02, extends SME relief to small mid-caps, simplifies AI literacy, bans AI that generates non-consensual sexual content or child sexual abuse material and allows special-category data to be processed to detect and correct bias [[r2]].\n\nThe deferral covers the Chapter III high-risk duties, not the transparency rules. The rest of the Digital Omnibus package (GDPR, ePrivacy, NIS2, Data Act) was not law in the cited law-firm note, so re-check before you claim it [[r3]]. The signature, publication and in-force dates come from that secondary source, because the EUR-Lex text did not load for scripted requests."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-l2-d1","title":"Which duties touch my system?","alt":"A decision flow. A prohibited practice stops the project. An Annex III use brings high-risk duties from 2027-12-02. Any system that talks to people or generates synthetic content has Article 50 duties from 2026-08-02. Everything else still has AI literacy and voluntary codes.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[AI system] --> B{Prohibited practice?}\n  B -->|yes| X[Do not ship]\n  B -->|no| C{Annex III use?}\n  C -->|yes| H[High-risk duties from 2027-12-02]\n  C -->|no| D{Chats or generates content?}\n  D -->|yes| T[Article 50 duties from 2026-08-02]\n  D -->|no| E[AI literacy and voluntary codes]\n  H --> D","caption":"A simplification for orientation, not legal advice. Check each system with counsel."},{"t":"table","caption":"Role decides the duty. One assistant can be both a provider of one system and a deployer of another.","head":["Role and system","Typical duty","Where"],"rows":[["Provider of a system that talks to people","Design it so people are told they interact with an AI system unless that is obvious, clearly and at the latest at the first interaction","Article 50(1) and 50(5) [[r6]]"],["Provider of a system that generates synthetic text, image, audio or video","Mark outputs in a machine-readable format so they are detectable. Systems on the market before 2026-08-02 have until 2026-12-02","Article 50(2) [[r6]]"],["Deployer of deep fake or public-interest text generation","Disclose that the content is artificially generated or manipulated. Text that went through human review or editorial control is exempt","Article 50(4) [[r6]]"],["Provider of a high-risk system","Keep automatic event logs, enable human oversight and report serious incidents","Articles 12, 14 and 73 [[r8]] [[r7]]"],["GPAI model provider","The voluntary Code of Practice (2025-07-10) is a recognised way to show compliance. Transparency and Copyright cover Article 53, Safety and Security covers Article 55","Code of Practice [[r9]]"]]},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Prohibited practices","value":"EUR 35M or 7%","note":"Of worldwide annual turnover. Undertakings pay the higher figure"},{"label":"Other operator obligations","value":"EUR 15M or 3%","note":"Same higher-figure rule"},{"label":"Incorrect information to authorities","value":"EUR 7.5M or 1%","note":"SMEs, start-ups and small mid-caps pay the lower figure"},{"label":"Annex III high-risk date","value":"2027-12-02","note":"Not 2026-08-02. Check any older slide"}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"The roadmap shows Annex III high-risk compliance due on 2026-08-02.","cause":"It was written before Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 entered into force on 2026-07-27.","fix":"Re-baseline to 2027-12-02 for Annex III and 2028-08-02 for Annex I. Keep Article 50 on 2026-08-02."},{"symptom":"A team says a deployer has no duties.","cause":"Duties differ by role, not by size. Deployers disclose deep fakes and public-interest text, and a serious incident clock runs from when the provider or deployer becomes aware.","fix":"Map every system to provider or deployer, per product, and record it in the inventory."},{"symptom":"A chatbot launched before 2026-08-02 shows no AI disclosure.","cause":"The transition covers Article 50(2) marking until 2026-12-02, not the Article 50(1) disclosure to people.","fix":"Disclose in the interface now and schedule the machine-readable marking before 2026-12-02."},{"symptom":"A sales deck calls the GPAI Code of Practice a legal requirement.","cause":"The code is voluntary, a way to demonstrate compliance with Articles 53 and 55.","fix":"Say voluntary, and map the code's chapters to the obligations you meet."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Date every claim. Say that Annex III moved to 2027-12-02, that Article 50 and enforcement for GPAI began on 2026-08-02, that the Omnibus date facts come from a secondary source and that you would confirm the legal text with counsel. Then say whether you are a provider or a deployer."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A roadmap says Annex III high-risk compliance is due on 2026-08-02. What changed?","options":["Nothing, the date stands","The Omnibus removed the high-risk duties entirely","Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 moved it to 2027-12-02","The Omnibus moved Article 50 transparency to 2027-12-02 as well"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Digital Omnibus on AI, in force since 2026-07-27, moved Annex III to 2027-12-02 and Annex I to 2028-08-02. It did not remove the duties, and the deferral is limited to Chapter III high-risk duties, so Article 50 transparency still applied from 2026-08-02.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w17-l2-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A company with EUR 2,000,000,000 worldwide annual turnover commits a prohibited practice. What is the maximum fine under Article 99, in EUR millions?","answer":140,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"EUR million","hint":"For an undertaking the higher of the two figures applies.","explain":"Prohibited practices carry up to EUR 35 million or 7% of worldwide annual turnover. Seven percent of EUR 2 billion is EUR 140 million, which is higher than EUR 35 million, and undertakings pay the higher figure. An SME or start-up would pay the lower of the two.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r4"},{"id":"w17-l2-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each date to what applies from it.","pairs":[{"l":"2025-02-02","r":"Prohibitions and AI literacy"},{"l":"2025-08-02","r":"General-purpose AI obligations"},{"l":"2026-08-02","r":"Article 50 transparency rules, and enforcement for GPAI and prohibitions"},{"l":"2027-12-02","r":"Annex III high-risk rules"}],"explain":"Prohibitions and AI literacy came first, GPAI obligations followed on 2025-08-02, Article 50 and enforcement started on 2026-08-02, and the Omnibus moved Annex III to 2027-12-02.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w17-l2-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A text-generating assistant was placed on the market on 2026-06-01 with no machine-readable marking of its output. By when must Article 50(2) marking be in place?","options":["2026-08-02","2026-12-02","2027-12-02","2028-08-02"],"answer":1,"explain":"Systems placed on the market before 2026-08-02 get until 2026-12-02 for Article 50(2) marking. 2026-08-02 is when Article 50 applies to new systems, and 2027-12-02 and 2028-08-02 are the Annex III and Annex I high-risk dates, which are a different obligation.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r6","r7","r8","r9"],"takeaways":["Date every claim. Annex III high-risk moved to 2027-12-02 and Annex I to 2028-08-02 after 2026-07-27.","Article 50 transparency and GPAI enforcement began on 2026-08-02. Marking has a transition to 2026-12-02.","Duties follow your role as provider or deployer, per system.","The GPAI Code of Practice is voluntary. Fines reach EUR 35M or 7% for prohibited practices.","Name the secondary sources in your answer and confirm legal text with counsel."]},{"id":"w17-l3","order":3,"title":"Law, guidance, standard or rulebook?","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A customer asks for ISO 42001, a regulator for EU AI Act evidence and the board for NIST alignment. Decide which framework you adopt first, say what each one is and is not, and decide what you will not claim.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four kinds of document, often confused","level":2,"id":"four-kinds-of-document-often-confused"},{"t":"table","caption":"What each framework is. Dates and statuses checked 2026-10-13.","head":["Framework","Kind","Binding or certifiable","Covers","Date and status"],"rows":[["EU AI Act","Regulation","Binding in the EU. Not a certificate","Prohibitions, GPAI, transparency, high-risk systems","In force since 2024-08-01, staged to 2028-08-02 [[r1]]"],["NIST AI RMF 1.0","Voluntary framework","Neither","Govern, Map, Measure, Manage","Released 2023-01-26. NIST says it is being revised [[r10]]"],["NIST AI 600-1","Voluntary GenAI profile","Neither","Twelve GenAI risks with suggested actions","Released 2024-07-26 [[r11]]"],["ISO/IEC 42001:2023","Management system standard","Certifiable, voluntary","An organization's AI management system (AIMS), Plan-Do-Check-Act","Published December 2023 [[r12]]"],["Microsoft Responsible AI Standard v2","One company's internal standard","Neither","Six goal groups, including human oversight and control","Published June 2022 [[r13]]"],["GPAI Code of Practice","Voluntary code","Neither","Transparency, Copyright, Safety and Security chapters","Published 2025-07-10 [[r9]]"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"p","md":"A **regulation** says what you must do in a jurisdiction. A **guidance framework** such as the NIST AI RMF gives a shared vocabulary and practices and is voluntary. A **management system standard** such as ISO/IEC 42001 lets an independent body certify that your organization runs an AI management system. An **internal standard** turns principles into engineering requirements.\n\nThe common mistake is to mix them. An ISO 42001 certificate covers the organization's management system, not the behaviour of one chatbot, and NIST AI RMF is not a regulation. NIST AI 600-1 names twelve GenAI risks: CBRN information or capabilities, confabulation, dangerous, violent or hateful content, data privacy, environmental impacts, harmful bias and homogenization, human-AI configuration, information integrity, information security, intellectual property, obscene, degrading or abusive content, and value chain and component integration. Prompt injection and data poisoning sit under information security."},{"t":"h","text":"Pick the first framework","level":2,"id":"pick-the-first-framework"},{"t":"dials","id":"w17-l3-framework","title":"Which framework do you adopt first?","prompt":"Set where you sell, whether customers ask for a certificate and how many teams build AI. The first choice changes. Most organizations end up with a combination.","dials":[{"id":"jur","label":"Jurisdiction","values":[{"id":"eu","label":"EU market"},{"id":"other","label":"Elsewhere, no binding AI law"},{"id":"global","label":"Global, mixed rules"}]},{"id":"demand","label":"Customer demand","values":[{"id":"none","label":"No formal ask"},{"id":"cert","label":"Procurement asks for a certificate"}]},{"id":"footprint","label":"Footprint","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One AI product"},{"id":"many","label":"Many teams and agents"}]}],"options":[{"id":"euact","name":"EU AI Act programme","summary":"Map duties by role and date and build evidence."},{"id":"nist","name":"NIST AI RMF with the GenAI profile","summary":"Govern, Map, Measure, Manage, with twelve GenAI risks."},{"id":"iso","name":"ISO/IEC 42001 certification","summary":"A certified AI management system."},{"id":"internal","name":"Internal standard in the Microsoft style","summary":"Goals, requirements and a Sensitive Uses review."},{"id":"checklist","name":"Lightweight checklist","summary":"The boring option, sized to one product."}],"scores":{"euact":{"jur":{"eu":{"s":2,"why":"Binding law for systems on the EU market."},"other":{"s":-1,"why":"No legal driver unless you serve EU users."},"global":{"s":1,"why":"Sets a high common bar."}},"demand":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"A legal duty needs no customer ask."},"cert":{"s":-1,"why":"It is not a certificate customers can request."}},"footprint":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"A shared programme pays off across systems."}}},"nist":{"jur":{"eu":{"s":0,"why":"Not a legal answer in the EU."},"other":{"s":2,"why":"A common voluntary reference outside the EU."},"global":{"s":1,"why":"A widely recognised baseline."}},"demand":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap to start."},"cert":{"s":-1,"why":"There is no NIST certification."}},"footprint":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Light to apply to one product."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"The Govern function scales across teams."}}},"iso":{"jur":{"eu":{"s":1,"why":"Supports compliance but does not prove it."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"Recognised internationally."},"global":{"s":2,"why":"One international standard for every region."}},"demand":{"none":{"s":-1,"why":"Costly without a customer ask."},"cert":{"s":2,"why":"Certifiable, which is what procurement asks for."}},"footprint":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"Heavy for one product."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"A management system pays off across teams."}}},"internal":{"jur":{"eu":{"s":0,"why":"Needs mapping to the law."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"Fits when no law drives you."},"global":{"s":1,"why":"One standard across regions."}},"demand":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"You control the rules."},"cert":{"s":-1,"why":"Not independently recognised."}},"footprint":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Shared goals and a Sensitive Uses review scale across teams."}}},"checklist":{"jur":{"eu":{"s":-2,"why":"Will not satisfy a legal duty."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"Fine at small scale."},"global":{"s":-1,"why":"Too thin for mixed regimes."}},"demand":{"none":{"s":2,"why":"Right-sized when nobody asks."},"cert":{"s":-2,"why":"Will fail a procurement review."}},"footprint":{"one":{"s":2,"why":"Right-sized for one product."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Does not scale to a fleet."}}}},"takeaway":"The law decides what you must do, a certificate decides what customers accept, and a voluntary framework decides how you organise the work. Pick the one your strongest driver demands, then borrow from the others."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"A sales deck says the chatbot is ISO 42001 certified.","cause":"The certificate covers the organization's AI management system, not the behaviour of one application.","fix":"Say the management system is certified and show application evidence: evals, oversight records and incident drills."},{"symptom":"NIST AI RMF is cited as a regulation, or without a version.","cause":"It is voluntary guidance, and NIST says 1.0 is being revised under the White House AI Action Plan.","fix":"Cite the version, call it voluntary and map its functions to your controls."},{"symptom":"The control mapping is complete but no one can show it operating.","cause":"Controls were listed without records that prove them.","fix":"Tie every control to evidence: approval logs, review dates, eval results and incident exercises."},{"symptom":"A customer asks for EU AI Act evidence and receives an ISO certificate.","cause":"Law and management system answer different questions.","fix":"Map duties by role and date. ISO 42001 can support compliance but does not prove it."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard v2","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The June 2022 external release has six goal groups: accountability, transparency, fairness, reliability and safety, privacy and security, and inclusiveness. Accountability includes goal A5, human oversight and control, and a Sensitive Uses review that is repeated at least annually. It is one company's internal rulebook, a useful template, not a certification [[r13]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the GPAI Code of Practice","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Published on 2025-07-10 and voluntary. The Commission and the AI Board confirmed it as an adequate voluntary compliance tool. The Transparency and Copyright chapters cover Article 53 for all GPAI providers, and the Safety and Security chapter covers Article 55 for models with systemic risk [[r9]]."}]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Say what each framework is before you pick one: law, guidance, certifiable standard or internal rulebook. Then say what you will not claim, for example that ISO 42001 certifies one chatbot or that NIST is binding."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does an ISO/IEC 42001 certificate show?","options":["An audited organization-level AI management system","The chatbot will not produce harmful or inaccurate answers in production","The system meets the EU AI Act high-risk requirements","The system passed the twelve risk tests in NIST AI 600-1"],"answer":0,"explain":"ISO/IEC 42001 specifies and certifies an AI management system for an organization, run as Plan-Do-Check-Act. It does not certify one application's behaviour, it does not prove EU AI Act compliance and NIST AI 600-1 is a voluntary profile with suggested actions, not a test suite.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w17-l3-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about the NIST AI RMF and AI 600-1.","options":["AI RMF 1.0 was released on 2023-01-26 and is voluntary.","AI 600-1 is the GenAI profile, released 2024-07-26, with twelve risks.","The four functions are Plan, Do, Check and Act.","NIST states that AI RMF 1.0 is being revised.","The AI RMF is a regulation with fines for non-compliance."],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The RMF is voluntary guidance with the functions Govern, Map, Measure and Manage, the GenAI profile lists twelve risks, and NIST says 1.0 is under revision. Plan-Do-Check-Act is the ISO management system cycle, and the RMF carries no fines.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w17-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A 15-person startup ships one chatbot to US customers. No customer asks for a certificate and no binding AI law applies. Which first step fits?","options":["Pursue ISO/IEC 42001 certification","Build a full EU AI Act compliance programme","Adopt the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard unchanged","A short checklist mapped to NIST functions"],"answer":3,"explain":"With one product, no certificate demand and no binding law, a lightweight checklist mapped to the NIST functions is right-sized and can grow later. ISO certification is heavy for one product, the EU programme answers a legal driver you do not have and a large internal standard copied unchanged adds process without a driver.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r10"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r9","r10","r11","r12","r13"],"takeaways":["Law, guidance, certifiable standard and internal rulebook answer different questions.","ISO/IEC 42001 certifies an organization's management system, not one chatbot.","NIST AI RMF is voluntary, 1.0 is under revision, and AI 600-1 lists twelve GenAI risks.","Pick the framework your strongest driver demands, then borrow from the others.","Evidence beats mapping: tie every control to a record."]},{"id":"w17-l4","order":4,"title":"A fleet needs a lifecycle","kind":"problem","minutes":22,"brief":"Agents multiply faster than reviews. Decide how every agent is inventoried, identified, permissioned, reviewed and retired, and which control plane holds the list, while the runtimes, clouds and licences behind your agents are still open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five stages from first commit to retirement","level":2,"id":"five-stages-from-first-commit-to-retirement"},{"t":"flow","title":"The agent governance lifecycle","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Inventory","md":"Register every agent with a sponsor, purpose, data and tool access, runtime and risk tier. If it is not listed, it is not governed.","tag":"registry"},{"title":"Identity","md":"Give each agent its own identity, not a shared account or a developer's credentials. Choose autonomous or on-behalf-of mode per agent.","tag":"entra"},{"title":"Permissions","md":"Grant least privilege through reviewed roles or access packages. Gate irreversible tools with approval.","tag":"access"},{"title":"Review","md":"Sponsors review purpose and access on a schedule, and again after a material change to model, tools or data.","tag":"review"},{"title":"Retirement","md":"Disable the identity, revoke credentials, delete memory and data under retention rules and close the registry entry.","tag":"retire"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Controls by stage on the Microsoft stack. Statuses checked 2026-10-13.","head":["Stage","Control","Microsoft option","Status","If you skip it"],"rows":[["Inventory","Registry with owners and types","Agent 365 registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center. Four types: Microsoft agents, external partner-built agents, published by your org, shared by creator [[r20]]","GA since 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, per-user licence [[r17]]","Shadow agents with no sponsor"],["Identity","Dedicated identity with a sponsor","Entra Agent ID: blueprint, agent identity and optional agent user. At least one sponsor per blueprint and agent identity [[r27]]","Agent ID is available to Entra customers. Agent users need the Frontier preview","Actions that cannot be traced to an agent"],["Permissions","Conditional Access and access packages","Conditional Access for agents and ID Governance access packages. Agents have no MFA [[r19]]","GA. Needs Microsoft 365 E7, or an Agent 365 licence with at least Entra P1 or Microsoft 365 E3","Standing over-privilege"],["Review","Sponsor access reviews and usage visibility","Sponsors decide retention and access reviews [[r18]]. Purview DSPM for AI shows AI usage [[r14]]. Defender for Cloud AI threat protection raises leakage, poisoning and jailbreak alerts [[r15]]","GA. Defender covers Azure OpenAI and Model Inference, text tokens only, Commercial cloud only","Stale purpose and permission creep"],["Retirement","Disable, soft-delete, hard-delete","Sponsors can disable and soft-delete. Owners can also re-enable, restore and hard-delete [[r18]]","Doc-stated","Zombie agents with live credentials"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"compare","title":"Where the inventory lives","intro":"Whatever you choose, the inventory must name an accountable sponsor for every agent.","options":[{"name":"Agent 365 registry","tag":"GA","summary":"Microsoft's control plane to observe, govern and secure agents, with the registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center.","pros":["Native integration for Microsoft platforms, an SDK for framework-built agents and registry synchronization for connected platforms such as Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock","Entra, Purview and Defender in one plane"],"cons":["Per-user licensing. Microsoft E5 is the best-fit prerequisite","Discovery of local and shadow agents through Defender and Intune was announced as preview on 2026-05-01"],"bestWhen":"Most agents run on Microsoft platforms or can register, and you already run Entra, Purview and Defender.","avoidWhen":"You have no Microsoft 365 footprint, or you operate in segments the Commercial GA announcement did not cover.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"GA 2026-05-01"},{"k":"Reach","v":"Microsoft plus connected"}]},{"name":"Entra Agent ID identities and sponsors only","summary":"Use agent identities with sponsors as the spine and add controls as budget allows.","pros":["Agent ID is available to Entra customers","Every agent gets an identity and a business sponsor"],"cons":["Conditional Access and ID Governance for agents need a Microsoft 365 E7 or Agent 365 licence","No registry views or observability on their own"],"bestWhen":"A small fleet, accountable identities needed first, licence budget still open.","avoidWhen":"Agents live outside Entra, or you need a registry and observability.","meta":[{"k":"Cost","v":"low to start"},{"k":"Reach","v":"Entra only"}]},{"name":"Self-run registry with CI gates","summary":"A catalogue you own, with pipeline checks that block unregistered deployments.","pros":["Works across clouds and runtimes","Policy as code"],"cons":["You build discovery, reviews and retirement","Drift between registry and reality"],"bestWhen":"A mixed or sovereign estate that Microsoft's plane does not reach.","avoidWhen":"A small Microsoft estate where licensing is simple.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"high"},{"k":"Reach","v":"anything you wire in"}]},{"name":"Per-team spreadsheets","summary":"The boring option: each team keeps its own list.","pros":["No tooling"],"cons":["No discovery or enforcement","Stale within weeks (design reasoning)"],"bestWhen":"A handful of agents in a pilot.","avoidWhen":"Any agent that can send data out or change records.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"lowest"},{"k":"Risk","v":"highest"}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"A review finds live agents whose sponsor left the company.","cause":"No succession process. Sponsors are required, but sponsorship has to be maintained when people move or leave.","fix":"Use supported groups or two sponsors, alert on departures and make sponsor transfer part of offboarding."},{"symptom":"An agent can read far more than its task needs.","cause":"Permissions were copied from a developer or granted once and never reviewed.","fix":"Use one identity per agent, least-privilege access packages and scheduled access reviews."},{"symptom":"An agent nobody registered sends customer email.","cause":"A shadow agent was built outside the registry, locally or as a SaaS agent.","fix":"Add discovery (preview as of 2026-05-01 in Agent 365), registry synchronization, a CI gate for deployments and an egress review."},{"symptom":"A retired agent's credentials still work.","cause":"Retirement stopped at hiding the agent. Credentials, memory and data stayed.","fix":"Disable the identity, revoke credentials on the blueprint, delete memory and data per retention and remove its tools."},{"symptom":"Behaviour changed after a model upgrade, but the last review was a year ago.","cause":"Reviews are tied to the calendar, not to change.","fix":"Trigger a review on material change, and keep a periodic review as the floor."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: Entra Agent ID objects and execution modes","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"A **blueprint** is a template that holds the credentials and permissions. An **agent identity** is a service principal with `servicePrincipalType` `ServiceIdentity` and has no credentials of its own: the blueprint uses its credentials to acquire tokens for the identities created from it. An optional **agent user** is a user object for the agent and needs the Frontier preview. Pick the execution mode first: **S2S** (the agent acts as itself with application permissions), **OBO** (the user is the token subject, the agent identity is the actor, delegated permissions) or **Agentic-User** (the agent acts as its own user account, Frontier preview) [[r27]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The Agent 365 SDK adds identity, governed tooling and notifications. It does not build, host or run the agent, and the Agent 365 Observability SDK is deprecated in favour of the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro."}]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Name the five stages, then say who is accountable at each one: a sponsor for purpose and lifecycle, an owner for technical changes. Mention the OWASP agentic risks that bear on it, ASI03 Identity and Privilege Abuse and ASI09 Human-Agent Trust Exploitation [[r22]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l4-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the agent governance lifecycle in order.","items":["Inventory the agent with an accountable sponsor","Give it its own identity, not a shared account","Grant least-privilege permissions through reviewed access","Review purpose and access on a schedule and after change","Retire it: disable, revoke credentials and delete data"],"explain":"You cannot give an identity to an agent you have not listed, you cannot scope permissions without an identity, and reviews only mean something once access exists. Retirement closes the loop by removing credentials and data so the agent cannot come back as a zombie.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w17-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A quarterly review finds agents that still run with live credentials after their business owner left. Which gap does this show?","options":["Missing Conditional Access on the agent","No sponsor succession and review process","The agent used OBO instead of S2S","The registry showed duplicate rows for one agent"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sponsors are required so every agent has an accountable business owner, but sponsorship must be maintained when a sponsor moves or leaves, and sponsors decide retention in access reviews. Conditional Access governs token issuance, the execution mode does not change who is accountable, and duplicate rows can be normal for a Copilot Studio agent.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r18"},{"id":"w17-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Your estate has Foundry agents plus agents on AWS Bedrock and Google Vertex AI, and you want one inventory without rebuilding agents. What fits?","options":["Agent 365 registry, with registry synchronization for the connected platforms","A Foundry project list, because Foundry hosts all agents","A shared spreadsheet maintained by each cloud team","Agent users in the Frontier preview for every agent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Agent 365 describes three integration paths: native for Microsoft platforms, the SDK for framework-built agents and registry synchronization for connected platforms such as Vertex AI and AWS Bedrock. A Foundry list does not cover other clouds, a spreadsheet gives no discovery or enforcement and agent users are an identity feature, not an inventory.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r14","r15","r17","r18","r19","r20","r22","r27"],"takeaways":["Five stages: inventory, identity, permissions, review, retirement. An unlisted agent is an ungoverned agent.","Every agent needs a sponsor for purpose and lifecycle. Plan for sponsor succession.","Agent 365 went GA on 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment. Shadow-agent discovery was announced as preview.","Agents have no MFA. Govern them with Conditional Access and access packages, which carry licence requirements.","Retire completely: identity, credentials, memory, data and registry entry."]},{"id":"w17-l5","order":5,"title":"When it goes wrong, and what you can show","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"An agent did something it should not, or a regulator asks what the system did last Tuesday. Decide how you detect, contain and classify AI incidents, what you record and what you disclose, before the day it happens.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"AI incidents need their own playbook","level":2,"id":"ai-incidents-need-their-own-playbook"},{"t":"p","md":"AI systems break the assumptions of classic incident response. Microsoft's guidance names four properties. **Taxonomy gaps**: harms such as dangerous instructions or misuse through natural language fall outside confidentiality, integrity and availability buckets. **Context-dependent severity**: the same model error matters differently for medical guidance than for trivia. **Ambiguous root cause**: training data, fine-tuning, retrieval inputs and user context interact. **Telemetry gaps**: standard monitoring misses output anomalies, and privacy-by-design defaults narrow the forensic record. A gap in a safety classifier can produce thousands of harmful outputs before a reviewer sees the first [[r21]].\n\nFor providers of high-risk systems Article 73 adds legal clocks. Report a serious incident immediately after establishing a causal link or its reasonable likelihood, and in any event within 15 days of becoming aware. The limit is 2 days for a widespread infringement or a serious incident under Article 3(49)(b), and 10 days after a death. An incomplete initial report is allowed, followed by a full one, and the provider must not alter the system in a way that could affect the evaluation of causes before informing the authorities [[r7]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Staged remediation","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"Stage 1: contain","md":"In the first hour block known-bad inputs, activate content filters and restrict access. Containment comes before the root-cause hunt.","tag":"first hour"},{"title":"Stage 2: expand","md":"In the first 24 hours broaden mitigations to related variants, using automated pattern analysis.","tag":"24 hours"},{"title":"Stage 3: fix at source","md":"Over days or weeks update classifiers, adjust the model and change the system. Allow and block lists are triage tools, not durable fixes.","tag":"days to weeks"},{"title":"Watch and protect","md":"Hold a watch period after each stage, because non-deterministic behaviour cannot be verified with one test pass. Rotate responders who face harmful content.","tag":"always"}]},{"t":"compare","title":"How to organise AI incident response","intro":"The documented pattern extends established practice. A separate team is the alternative at scale.","options":[{"name":"Treat it as a normal security incident","summary":"Route AI incidents through the existing security process unchanged.","pros":["No new process","One on-call rota"],"cons":["Harm categories fall into generic buckets","Severity by record count misses harm","No telemetry for output anomalies"],"bestWhen":"A pure confidentiality incident, such as a leaked credential.","avoidWhen":"Harmful, manipulated or misleading outputs.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"lowest"},{"k":"Coverage","v":"narrow"}]},{"name":"Extend the existing process","tag":"Documented pattern","summary":"Keep ownership, containment before investigation and clear communication, then add AI harm categories, domain-weighted severity, AI observability, staged remediation and responder protection.","pros":["Reuses what already works","One escalation path"],"cons":["Needs new telemetry and training","Logging must be reconciled with privacy before an incident"],"bestWhen":"A mature security team and AI features inside existing products.","avoidWhen":"Many models and no shared incident discipline.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"medium"},{"k":"Coverage","v":"broad"}]},{"name":"A separate AI safety response team","summary":"A specialist team owns model and content harms next to the security team.","pros":["Deep skill in model and content harms"],"cons":["Duplicated on-call and handoff gaps between teams"],"bestWhen":"A large organization with many models and content-facing products.","avoidWhen":"A small team, where handoffs cost more than specialisation gains.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"high"},{"k":"Coverage","v":"deep"}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Records to keep before the incident, and why.","head":["Record","Keeps","Needed for","Source"],"rows":[["Retrieval and decision log","Document ids, principal, filter hash, outcome","Reproducing a leak or a wrong answer without copying text","Design reasoning, see w07"],["Approval log","Who approved, the evidence shown, the time","Accountability and automation-bias reviews","Article 14 intent [[r5]]"],["Tool-call audit","Tool, arguments, result and the acting identity","Reconstructing what the agent did","Agentic risks such as ASI03 [[r22]]"],["Version record","Model, prompt, tool and policy versions at the time","Root cause across data, tuning, retrieval and context","Ambiguous root cause [[r21]]"],["Event logs","Automatic events over the system's lifetime","Risk identification and post-market monitoring","Article 12 for high-risk systems [[r8]]"],["Disclosure and marking","Proof people were told they use an AI system, and marking of generated content","Showing compliance with transparency rules","Article 50 [[r6]]"],["Incident timeline","Detection, severity, containment, communication, fix, watch periods","Article 73 reports and lessons learned","[[r7]] [[r21]]"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"After an incident nobody can say which documents or tools the agent used.","cause":"Privacy-by-design defaults turned content logging off and nobody logged identifiers.","fix":"Reconcile logging with privacy before an incident: ids and decisions always, content under a bounded policy."},{"symptom":"The team hunts for a root cause for days while the harmful output continues.","cause":"Containment was skipped, and root cause in AI systems is often ambiguous.","fix":"Contain in the first hour with block lists, filters and access limits, then investigate."},{"symptom":"A fix passes one test and the incident returns.","cause":"Non-deterministic behaviour was verified with a single pass.","fix":"Use watch periods after each stage and replay variants at volume."},{"symptom":"A first-hour block list is still the only fix three months later.","cause":"Tactical lists were treated as durable.","fix":"Schedule stage 3: classifier updates, model adjustments and systemic changes."},{"symptom":"Responders burn out after an incident with abusive content.","cause":"Exposure to harmful content was not planned for.","fix":"Rotate responders, schedule breaks, offer peer support and borrow practices from content moderation teams."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Say contain first, then investigate. Name the AI-specific gaps (taxonomy, severity by domain, root cause, telemetry), the three stages with their time boxes and the records you keep. For high-risk systems name the Article 73 deadlines: 15 days, 2 days and 10 days."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l5-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the response to an AI incident in order.","items":["Classify with AI-specific harm categories and domain-weighted severity","Contain in the first hour: block known-bad inputs, activate filters, restrict access","Expand mitigations to related variants within 24 hours","Fix at source over days or weeks: classifier and model changes"],"explain":"Classification sets severity and ownership, containment limits the damage in the first hour, expansion covers variants within 24 hours and the source fix takes days or weeks. Watch periods follow each stage because one test pass cannot verify non-deterministic behaviour.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r21"},{"id":"w17-l5-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A provider of a high-risk system becomes aware of a serious incident involving the death of a person. What is the latest number of days after becoming aware to submit the report?","answer":10,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"days","hint":"The general limit is 15 days, and death has its own shorter limit.","explain":"Article 73 sets 15 days as the general limit, 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident and 10 days after a death, always immediately after a causal link is established or suspected. An incomplete initial report is allowed to meet the deadline.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w17-l5-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After an incident the team cannot tell which documents the agent retrieved. The log holds the following. What explains the gap?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"request_id=9f2c user=svc-agent model=m-2026-09 status=200\nprompt: [not captured]  answer: [not captured]\nretrieval: [not captured]"},"options":["The model was too large to log","Containment ran before classification","A telemetry gap: privacy defaults dropped content and no identifiers were logged","The incident was a pure confidentiality incident"],"answer":2,"explain":"Microsoft's guidance lists telemetry gaps and warns that privacy-by-design defaults narrow the forensic record, so logging must be reconciled before an incident. Model size does not stop logging, the order of containment and classification does not remove records, and the missing retrieval trace is the gap regardless of incident type.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r21"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r5","r6","r7","r8","r21","r22"],"takeaways":["AI incidents have taxonomy, severity, root-cause and telemetry gaps that classic response misses.","Contain in the first hour, expand in 24 hours, fix at source over days or weeks, and watch after each stage.","High-risk providers report serious incidents within 15 days, or 2 days, or 10 days after a death.","Decide what you log before the incident: ids and decisions always, content under a bounded policy.","Rehearse the playbook, including responder protection."]},{"id":"w17-l6","order":6,"title":"Boss: govern the fleet","kind":"boss","minutes":22,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to set up governance for a fleet of agents, with every dial unspecified. Practise the five steps of Dial-and-Defend: clarify, list options, pick and flip, name failures, name metrics and rollout.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The interview prompt","level":2,"id":"the-interview-prompt"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["About 60 agents across Foundry, Copilot Studio and a self-hosted runtime in another cloud","A handful can send email or change customer records","One supports a hiring workflow"],"ask":["Which regulations and customers apply, and are we provider or deployer?","Which actions are irreversible or externally visible, and how many a day?","Who owns each agent, and which identity does it use?","Which platforms and licences do we hold?","What do we log today, and what incidents have we had?"],"tension":"Tight control slows teams, and loose control hides risk. Say which dial you trade and for which tier."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-l6-d1","title":"A governance loop for a fleet","alt":"An inventory sweep feeds a tiering step by action risk. Each tier gets its own oversight. Lifecycle reviews follow, evidence and incidents feed back into the tiers.","src":"flowchart LR\n  I[Inventory sweep] --> T[Tier by action risk]\n  T --> O[Oversight per tier]\n  O --> L[Lifecycle reviews]\n  L --> E[Evidence and incidents]\n  E --> T","caption":"Evidence from reviews and incidents moves agents between tiers."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-l6-q1","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select every dial you use to tier an agent's oversight.","options":["Reversibility of its actions","Reach of its actions","Which framework logo the vendor shows","Whether the use falls in an Annex III area","The agent's token cost per request"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Oversight follows what can go wrong and how bad it is: how reversible the action is, how many people or outside parties it reaches and whether the use is high-risk under the AI Act. A vendor logo and token cost say nothing about harm, although cost matters elsewhere in capacity planning.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"},{"id":"w17-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Reviewers approve 98% of agent requests in about three seconds each. Which change fits best?","options":["Add a second approver to every request","Remove the approval step because it is ignored","Tier by reversibility and seed known-bad items","Hire more reviewers to shorten the queue"],"answer":2,"explain":"A near-total approval rate at three seconds signals rubber-stamping. Tiering moves reversible actions to sampled review, keeps approval for the risky slice and seeded bad items measure whether reviewers still catch errors. A second approver doubles the friction, removing the gate drops oversight and more reviewers do not fix the screen or the volume.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5"},{"id":"w17-l6-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the first moves of a governance programme for an unknown fleet in order.","items":["Sweep and inventory every agent with a sponsor","Tier agents by reversibility, reach, data and regulatory class","Set oversight and permissions per tier","Schedule reviews, evidence collection and incident drills"],"explain":"You cannot tier what you have not found, you cannot set oversight before you know the tier, and reviews and drills only make sense once oversight and permissions exist.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r17"}],"defend":{"prompt":"You are asked to set up governance for a fleet of 60 agents. Some run on Foundry, some in Copilot Studio and a few are self-hosted in another cloud. A handful can send email or change customer records, and one supports a hiring workflow. Walk me through how you would govern them.","timebox":"6 min","clarify":["Which regulations and customers apply: EU users, procurement that asks for ISO/IEC 42001, sector rules?","Are we the provider or the deployer of each system, and which uses fall in Annex III areas such as employment?","Which actions are irreversible or externally visible, and how many happen per day?","Who owns each agent today, and which identity and credentials does each one use?","Which platforms and licences do we hold: Agent 365, Entra, Purview, Defender?","What do we log today, and what incidents or near misses have we had?"],"options":[{"name":"Central control plane: Agent 365 with Entra Agent ID, Purview and Defender","when":"Most agents run on Microsoft platforms or can register, and the licences are in place.","tradeoff":"Per-user licensing, other clouds need registry synchronization or the SDK, and shadow-agent discovery was announced as preview."},{"name":"Federated model: teams own agents, a shared policy-as-code gate and a lightweight registry","when":"A mixed or sovereign estate that no single control plane reaches.","tradeoff":"You build discovery, reviews and retirement, and registry drift is a constant risk."},{"name":"Certification-led programme: ISO/IEC 42001 first","when":"Procurement asks for a certificate and many teams build AI.","tradeoff":"Heavy to run, and it certifies the organization's management system, not each agent."},{"name":"Per-team checklists","when":"A handful of low-risk agents during a pilot.","tradeoff":"No discovery or enforcement, and stale within weeks."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask which laws and customers apply, whether we are provider or deployer per system, which actions are irreversible or externally visible, who owns each agent and which identity it uses, which platforms and licences we hold, and what we log today."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. Options.** A central control plane (Agent 365 with Entra Agent ID, Purview and Defender), a federated model with a shared gate and a lightweight registry, a certification-led programme, or per-team checklists as the boring baseline."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choice for assumed dials.** Assume mixed platforms, EU users, five agents that send email or change records and one hiring agent in an Annex III area. I would start with a 30 day inventory sweep, tier every agent by reversibility, reach, data sensitivity and regulatory class, then set oversight per tier:"},{"t":"table","head":["Tier","Example","Oversight","Why"],"rows":[["0","Read-only internal assistant","Log and alert","Reversible, low reach"],["1","Reversible internal writes","Sampled review with undo","High volume, easy undo"],["2","Sends email or changes customer records","Approval before action, gated by action class","Externally visible or costly to undo"],["3","Hiring workflow (Annex III employment)","Approval with evidence and an override and stop control, event logs and incident readiness","High-risk class, duties from 2027-12-02"]]},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Every agent has a registered sponsor. No sponsor, no deployment, enforced by a CI gate.","One identity per agent, autonomous or on-behalf-of mode chosen per agent, least-privilege access, Conditional Access where licensed.","Sponsor reviews on a schedule and after material change, and a Sensitive Uses style review for tier 3 at least annually.","A retirement checklist: disable the identity, revoke credentials, delete memory and data, close the registry entry."]},{"t":"p","md":"**Frameworks and law.** I would date duties by role: Article 50 disclosure from 2026-08-02, marking for systems already on the market by 2026-12-02 and Annex III by 2027-12-02. ISO/IEC 42001 only if procurement demands it, and NIST AI RMF with AI 600-1 as the working vocabulary. **Incidents.** Staged remediation: contain in the first hour, expand in 24 hours, fix at source over days or weeks, with Article 73 clocks of 15, 2 or 10 days for any high-risk system."},{"t":"p","md":"**4 and 5. Failure modes, metrics and rollout.** I would watch for orphaned agents, permission creep, shadow agents and rubber-stamped approvals. Metrics: share of agents with a sponsor and their own identity, access-review completion, approval latency and seeded-error catch rate, time to contain in drills and unregistered agents found per month. Rollout: sweep, tier, enforce gates for tiers 2 and 3, then extend."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Say the status out loud","md":"Agent 365 was generally available on 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment. Discovery of local and shadow agents was announced as preview, and Entra agent users need the Frontier preview program [[r24]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip it.** An all-Microsoft estate with licences flips me to the central plane. A procurement certificate requirement with many teams flips me to ISO/IEC 42001 first. A handful of low-risk agents flips me to checklists. A surge in irreversible actions flips me to automated policy checks and tighter triggers rather than more reviewers."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Clarifies jurisdiction, role, risky uses, owners, platforms and incident history before designing","w":3},{"pt":"Tiers agents by reversibility, reach, data and regulatory class, not by team","w":3},{"pt":"Sets different oversight per tier and avoids approval everywhere or logging everywhere","w":3},{"pt":"Covers the full lifecycle with sponsors, reviews and retirement","w":2},{"pt":"Dates EU AI Act duties correctly and justifies the framework choice","w":2},{"pt":"Covers incident response: staged remediation, records and Article 73 for high-risk systems","w":2},{"pt":"Names metrics and a rollout order","w":2},{"pt":"Labels preview features and states what would flip the choice","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"Reviewers approve 98% of requests in three seconds. What do you change?","a":"That is rubber-stamping. Show evidence and arguments, seed known-bad items and track the catch rate, and move reversible actions to sampled review so reviewers face only the risky slice."},{"q":"A customer asks for ISO/IEC 42001 evidence. What do you say?","a":"It certifies our AI management system, not one agent. I show the certificate scope plus agent-level evidence: registry, reviews, approval logs, evals and incident drills."},{"q":"An agent's sponsor leaves the company. What happens?","a":"Offboarding flags sponsored agents, a new sponsor is assigned (supported groups or two sponsors), and an access review runs. Agents without a sponsor are disabled until someone accepts accountability."}],"redFlags":["Applies one oversight level to every agent.","Treats an ISO certificate or the NIST framework as proof that each agent is safe or legal.","Quotes 2026-08-02 as the Annex III high-risk date.","Has no inventory, sponsor or retirement step, or no incident drill."]},"refs":["r1","r10","r12","r17","r21","r24","r25","r26"],"takeaways":["Tier agents by reversibility, reach, data and regulatory class, then set oversight per tier.","A sponsor, an identity, reviewed access and a retirement step for every agent.","Date the law by role, and say what ISO 42001 and NIST are and are not.","Contain in the first hour, expand in 24 hours, fix at source, and keep the records.","Study next: Govern AI usage with Microsoft Purview and the responsible generative AI module in Foundry."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w17-t01","order":1,"group":"Oversight","title":"Human oversight models","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Human oversight ranges from approving each action before it happens, through supervising with a stop control, to setting policy and reviewing samples afterwards. The right level follows the action's reversibility, reach and regulatory class, and costs friction.","useIf":"You must decide how much a person should be involved in what an AI system or agent does.","avoidIf":"You plan to bolt on a yes/no button and call it oversight: approval without evidence or authority is theatre.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three models are in common use, though sources define them loosely, so state your own definitions. **In the loop**: a person approves or makes each decision before it takes effect. **On the loop**: the system acts and a person monitors, with the power to interrupt or stop. **Over the loop**: people set policy and boundaries, then audit logs and review samples afterwards. The same system can mix them by action class: reads run freely, reversible writes are sampled, irreversible actions wait for approval."},{"t":"p","md":"Law points the same way. For high-risk systems, EU AI Act Article 14 requires oversight commensurate with risk, autonomy and context. People must be able to understand the system's limits, stay aware of automation bias, interpret outputs, decide not to use or override an output, and interrupt with a stop control. For biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a), two competent people must verify separately before action [[r5]]. Deployers must assign oversight to people with competence, training, authority and support [[tr1]]. Meta's Agents Rule of Two adds a design rule: if a session needs untrusted input, sensitive access and the ability to change state or communicate externally, require human approval or an equivalent [[r23]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Oversight models and their price","head":["Model","Person does","Friction","Fails when"],"rows":[["In the loop (approve first)","Decides each action","High: latency, reviewer cost","Rubber-stamping, queue backlog"],["On the loop (supervise, escalate)","Watches, steps in on triggers","Medium","Triggers are wrong, no stop control"],["Over the loop (set policy, audit)","Sets bounds, reviews samples and logs","Low per action","Harm is irreversible before the audit"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Approval is not oversight by itself","md":"A reviewer who sees no evidence, has no authority to refuse or is rewarded for speed will approve everything. Show arguments and sources, seed known-bad items and measure the catch rate. The Agent Framework binds a local approval response to a pending request in the same session by default, and `disable_approval_response_binding` opts out [[r16]]."}],"whereToUse":["Agents that send email, change records, move money or deploy code","High-risk systems under the EU AI Act, where Article 14 and Article 26 apply","Customer-facing assistants that escalate on low confidence or policy triggers","Bulk automation, using sampled review with an easy undo","Sessions that combine untrusted input, sensitive data and external effects"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Approve first when actions are irreversible or externally visible","Supervise and escalate when volume is moderate and triggers are reliable","Audit samples when actions are reversible, low-risk and high-volume","Require two reviewers where the law or the stakes demand it"],"avoid":["Approving every low-risk action: reviewers learn to click through","Relying on after-the-fact audit for irreversible harm","Putting oversight on people with no authority or competence to override"],"flips":["The action becomes irreversible: move from sampled audit to approval","Volume rises tenfold: replace blanket approval with risk-tiered gates","The system is classified high-risk: add the Article 14 capabilities and a stop control"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Oversight levels compared","head":["Level","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Approve before action","Irreversible, external, high-value","High volume, low risk","Latency, fatigue, rubber-stamping"],["Escalate on trigger","Most cases are routine, some are not","Triggers are hard to define","Missed triggers, noisy alerts"],["Sampled review after the fact","Reversible, high-volume","Harm cannot be undone","Defects found late"],["Audit log only","Low risk, internal","Anything with external effect","Accountability without prevention"],["No human in the loop","Fully reversible, trivial stakes","Regulated or consequential use","No check on drift or misuse"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Approval rate is 99.8% and median review time is a few seconds","cause":"Rubber-stamping from automation bias, no evidence shown or pressure to clear the queue","fix":"Show arguments and sources, seed known-bad items, track catch rate and rotate reviewers"},{"symptom":"An approval is replayed or fabricated","cause":"Approval responses are not bound to the pending request, user and session","fix":"Keep the default binding in Agent Framework and bind approvals to request, user and session [[r16]]"},{"symptom":"A write path bypasses the gate","cause":"Approval was attached to one tool, not to the action class","fix":"Gate by action class and arguments, so another tool cannot cause the same effect"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t01-s1","title":"An agent that emails customers","situation":"An agent drafts and sends account emails to customers. About 200 emails go out per day. A sent email cannot be recalled.","constraints":["Externally visible and irreversible","Moderate volume","Reviewers are trained support staff"],"unknowns":["Can the reviewer see the sources and the diff?","How fast must emails go out?","Is any email class low-risk enough to skip approval?"],"question":"What oversight model fits?","options":[{"name":"Approve before sending, with the draft, recipient and source evidence on screen","verdict":"best","why":"The action is irreversible and public, and 200 per day is a manageable queue.","tradeoff":"Adds minutes of latency and reviewer time."},{"name":"Send first and sample-review afterwards","verdict":"poor","why":"A wrong or injected email cannot be taken back.","tradeoff":"Right for reversible actions such as internal tags."},{"name":"Escalate only when the model reports low confidence","verdict":"good","why":"Cuts review load.","tradeoff":"Model confidence is a weak signal, and a prompt-injected agent can be confident and wrong."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume reaches 20,000 per day","then":"Tier by risk: approve high-value or new-recipient emails, template the rest."},{"if":"Emails are internal and recallable","then":"Sampled review with an undo is enough."}],"takeaway":"Irreversible and external means approve first."},{"id":"w17-t01-s2","title":"Bulk tagging of documents","situation":"An agent applies metadata tags to 50,000 internal documents a day. Tags can be changed at any time and affect search ranking only.","constraints":["Reversible","High volume","Low blast radius"],"unknowns":["How are wrong tags detected?","Who owns the tag taxonomy?"],"question":"How much human oversight?","options":[{"name":"Sampled review with an easy undo and an alert on drift in tag distribution","verdict":"best","why":"Per-item approval would be impossible and adds little safety for reversible tags.","tradeoff":"Some wrong tags live until the sample or alert catches them."},{"name":"Approve every tag","verdict":"poor","why":"50,000 approvals a day produces rubber-stamping, not safety.","tradeoff":"Right for a small, high-value set."},{"name":"Audit log only","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and keeps records.","tradeoff":"No active check, so drift goes unseen."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tags drive access control","then":"They are no longer low-risk: add approval for permission-affecting tags."},{"if":"Error rate in samples exceeds a threshold","then":"Pause the agent and escalate."}],"takeaway":"Reversible and high-volume means sample, with undo."},{"id":"w17-t01-s3","title":"A system classed as high-risk","situation":"A deployer uses an AI system that ranks applicants for a service. The deployer has classified it as high-risk under the EU AI Act. A reviewer currently clicks Accept on the ranking.","constraints":["Regulated high-risk system","Decisions affect people","Reviewers are overloaded"],"unknowns":["Do reviewers have competence, training and authority to override?","Is there a stop control?","Are reviewers told how the system can be wrong?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Redesign review: show evidence and limits, allow override and stop, assign trained reviewers with authority, log decisions","verdict":"best","why":"Article 14 and 26 expect understanding, awareness of automation bias, override and interruption, with competent and empowered people.","tradeoff":"Slower reviews and training cost."},{"name":"Keep the Accept button and add an audit log","verdict":"poor","why":"A log without real review does not meet the intent of oversight.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for non-regulated, low-stakes use."},{"name":"Remove the human and publish accuracy numbers","verdict":"poor","why":"It drops the oversight the Act expects and shifts risk to affected people.","tradeoff":"Not a fit for high-risk use."}],"flips":[{"if":"Biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a) is involved","then":"Two competent people must verify separately."},{"if":"The classification is not high-risk","then":"Oversight can be lighter, but keep escalation for harm."}],"takeaway":"In high-risk use, oversight is a capability, not a button."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t01-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which capabilities does EU AI Act Article 14 expect oversight people to have for high-risk systems?","options":["Understand the system's limits and stay aware of automation bias","Decide not to use or override an output","Interrupt the system with a stop control","Approve outputs without access to the reasoning inputs"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Article 14 lists understanding limits, automation-bias awareness, interpretation, override and a stop control. Approving without evidence is the failure it guards against [[r5]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t01-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A review queue shows 99.8% approvals with a median review time of 4 seconds. What is the most likely issue and first fix?","options":["The model is perfect: raise volume","Reviewers are too slow: remove review","Rubber-stamping: seed known-bad items and show evidence"],"answer":2,"explain":"Near-total approval at seconds per item signals automation bias. Seeding bad items measures real catch rate. Perfection is unlikely and removing review drops the control.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t01-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most clearly moves an action from sampled audit to approve-first?","options":["Volume doubles","The action becomes irreversible or externally visible","The model gets a higher benchmark score"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reversibility and reach choose the family of brake. Volume adjusts it, and benchmark scores do not change blast radius.","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"In, on and over the loop?","back":"In: a person approves each decision. On: the system acts and a person can interrupt. Over: people set policy and audit samples afterwards. Definitions vary by source."},{"front":"What does Article 14 require of oversight people?","back":"Understand limits, stay aware of automation bias, interpret output, override and interrupt with a stop control. Two competent people for Annex III point 1(a)."},{"front":"What is the Agents Rule of Two?","back":"In one session, avoid combining untrusted input, sensitive access and external effects. If all three are needed, require human approval."}],"related":["w17-t02","w17-t03","w17-t04","w17-l1","w10-t10"],"refs":["r5","r16","r23","tr1"]},{"id":"w17-t02","order":2,"group":"Oversight","title":"Approval gates and escalation","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An approval gate pauses an agent action until a person decides. Tier actions by reversibility and reach, gate the risky tiers, define who reviews and what happens on timeout, and bind each approval to the request it answers.","useIf":"An agent can take actions with external, irreversible or high-value effects.","avoidIf":"Every tool call would need approval: reviewers will rubber-stamp, so tier the actions first.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Start by **tiering actions** on four dials: reversibility, blast radius, data sensitivity and external visibility. A read-only internal action runs freely. A reversible internal write runs with logging and sampled review. An external or hard-to-undo action needs one approval. An irreversible, high-value or regulated action needs two reviewers and evidence. Tier by **action class and arguments**, not by tool name: a refund of 5 and a refund of 5,000 are different risks, and a second tool that sends the same email must fall in the same tier."},{"t":"p","md":"In Microsoft Agent Framework a Python tool opts in with `@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")`, and the run result exposes `user_input_requests`. In .NET you wrap the function in `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction` and the response carries `ToolApprovalRequestContent`. By default a local approval response authorises a call only when it matches a pending request recorded in the same `AgentSession`; `disable_approval_response_binding` opts out and can let a fabricated or replayed response approve a privileged call [[r16]]. Define **escalation** too: triggers (policy hit, low confidence, novelty, amount, anomaly), a named queue with an SLA, and a **fail-closed** default so an unanswered request means the action does not run."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t02-d1","title":"A tiered approval path","alt":"The agent proposes an action, a policy check routes low tiers straight to execution and high tiers to an approval queue. A reviewer approves to execute or rejects, and timeouts do not execute. Everything is logged.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent proposes action] --> P{Policy check}\n  P -->|tier 0 or 1| X[Execute and log]\n  P -->|tier 2 or 3| Q[Approval queue]\n  Q --> R[Reviewer sees evidence]\n  R -->|approve| X\n  R -->|reject or timeout| N[Do not execute]"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Opting a tool into approval (illustrative)","code":"@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -> str:\n    ...\n\n# The run result exposes user_input_requests. Resume with the same\n# session so the approval binds to the pending request.","caption":"Pair the built-in hook with policy code that decides which calls need it."}],"whereToUse":["Payment, refund and procurement tools","Outbound email, messaging and publishing actions","Infrastructure changes and data deletion","Cross-tenant or cross-boundary data movement","Any session that combines untrusted input, sensitive data and external effects [[r23]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The action is irreversible or externally visible","Prompt injection could steer the agent into it","Amounts or data sensitivity cross a threshold","A regulator or contract expects a human decision"],"avoid":["Gating reads and trivial reversible writes","Approvals with no evidence, authority or time to decide","Auto-approve on timeout"],"flips":["The reviewer queue exceeds its SLA: raise thresholds or add reviewers, never auto-approve","Actions become reversible with an undo: move down a tier","A new tool exposes the same effect: classify it in the same tier"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Gate designs compared","head":["Design","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Per-tool approval flag","Few tools, clear risk per tool","Risk depends on arguments or a second tool offers the same effect","Gaps and over-approval"],["Policy-as-code tiers on action class and arguments","Many tools, mixed risk","Policy is hard to specify","Policy bugs, maintenance"],["Two-person approval","High-value, regulated, irreversible","Volume or latency is tight","Slow, costly"],["Escalate on trigger only","Most actions are routine","Triggers miss novel failures","Silent bad actions"],["Sandbox and dry-run first","Effects can be previewed","Effects are external and real","Preview may not match reality"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A tool is gated but the same effect still happens","cause":"Approval is attached to one tool, and another tool or path reaches the same outcome","fix":"Gate by action class and arguments, and test alternative paths"},{"symptom":"Requests pile up and approvals are rushed","cause":"Too many low-risk actions reach the queue","fix":"Tier, raise thresholds for reversible actions, and measure catch rate on seeded bad items"},{"symptom":"Actions run when nobody answered","cause":"Timeout falls open to approve","fix":"Fail closed: no decision means no action, with a visible expiry and retry path"},{"symptom":"A fabricated approval executes a call","cause":"Approval binding to the pending request was disabled","fix":"Keep the default binding and bind to request, user and session [[r16]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t02-s1","title":"Twelve tools, three of them dangerous","situation":"An internal agent has twelve tools. Eight read data, three write reversible internal records, and one sends payments up to a large limit.","constraints":["Interactive use","Reviewers are limited","Payments are irreversible"],"unknowns":["What is the payment threshold for two-person approval?","Can internal writes be undone?"],"question":"How do you set gates?","options":[{"name":"Tier by action: reads free, internal writes logged with sampled review, payments gated by amount with two-person approval above a threshold","verdict":"best","why":"Effort goes where harm is irreversible, and the queue stays small enough to be reviewed carefully.","tradeoff":"Policy and thresholds need upkeep."},{"name":"Approve every tool call","verdict":"poor","why":"Eight read tools flood the queue, and reviewers start clicking through.","tradeoff":"Right for a tiny tool set that is all high-risk."},{"name":"Gate only the payment tool by name","verdict":"good","why":"Protects the biggest risk.","tradeoff":"Misses effects reachable by other tools and amounts that matter at lower values."}],"flips":[{"if":"Another tool can trigger the same payment indirectly","then":"Gate by effect, not by tool."},{"if":"Internal writes cannot be undone","then":"Move them up a tier."}],"takeaway":"Tier by effect and arguments, not by tool name."},{"id":"w17-t02-s2","title":"No reviewer at 3 a.m.","situation":"Overnight, an agent proposes a high-value action and the approval queue has no active reviewer for six hours.","constraints":["Action is irreversible","Business prefers speed","A rota exists but is thin overnight"],"unknowns":["Can the action wait until morning?","Is there an on-call path for urgent cases?"],"question":"What happens to the request?","options":[{"name":"Fail closed: hold the request, notify on-call for urgent cases and expire visibly","verdict":"best","why":"No decision means no irreversible action, and the owner still gets a path for urgent work.","tradeoff":"Delay and a possible missed business window."},{"name":"Auto-approve after one hour","verdict":"poor","why":"It turns the gate into a delay, and attackers can wait out the timer.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for reversible, low-value actions."},{"name":"Let the agent run with a smaller amount","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces exposure.","tradeoff":"Splitting amounts can be gamed and changes the business outcome."}],"flips":[{"if":"The action is reversible with an undo","then":"Run it, log it and review in the morning."},{"if":"Overnight volume is high","then":"Staff a follow-the-sun rota."}],"takeaway":"A gate that fails open is not a gate."},{"id":"w17-t02-s3","title":"Untrusted input, private data, external send","situation":"An agent reads inbound customer emails, can search private account data, and can send replies. A crafted email could steer it.","constraints":["All three Rule of Two properties are needed","Volume is moderate","Replies are visible to customers"],"unknowns":["Can a leg be removed, such as sending without human review?","How are injection attempts detected?"],"question":"What control do you add first?","options":[{"name":"Require human approval on outbound replies that include private data","verdict":"best","why":"The Rule of Two says approval is the fallback when untrusted input, sensitive access and external effects are all needed.","tradeoff":"Adds latency and reviewer workload."},{"name":"Rely on a system prompt that says to ignore instructions in emails","verdict":"poor","why":"It is a probabilistic control and attackers bypass classifiers.","tradeoff":"Useful as one layer, never the only one."},{"name":"Remove private data access and reply from templates","verdict":"good","why":"Removes a trifecta leg.","tradeoff":"Reduces what the agent can do for customers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Replies can be restricted to non-sensitive templates","then":"Removing a leg beats approval."},{"if":"Volume grows past reviewer capacity","then":"Gate by data sensitivity and recipient novelty."}],"takeaway":"If you cannot remove a trifecta leg, put a person on the exit."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t02-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the approval path in order.","items":["Agent proposes an action","Policy classifies tier by action and arguments","Reviewer sees evidence and decides","Action executes only on approval","Decision and evidence are logged"],"explain":"Classification precedes review, execution follows an explicit decision, and the audit record closes the loop. A timeout leads to no execution, not to approval.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Why does Agent Framework bind an approval response to a pending request in the same session by default?","options":["To make approvals faster","So a fabricated or replayed response cannot approve a privileged call","To store approvals in the model"],"answer":1,"explain":"The default binding stops replayed or invented approvals. `disable_approval_response_binding` removes that protection [[r16]]. Speed and storage are unrelated.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t02-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A tool needs approval but the reviewer queue is behind. Which response is acceptable?","options":["Auto-approve after a timeout","Disable logging to speed up","Add reviewers or raise thresholds for reversible actions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Capacity and tiering fix backlog. Auto-approve and removing logs weaken the control and accountability.","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you declare approval in Agent Framework?","back":"Python: `@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")` with `user_input_requests` on the result. .NET: wrap in `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction`, response carries `ToolApprovalRequestContent`."},{"front":"Why tier by action class, not tool name?","back":"Risk depends on effect and arguments. Another tool or a larger amount can reach the same harm past a per-tool flag."},{"front":"What should a timeout do?","back":"Fail closed: no decision means no action. Expire visibly and give urgent cases an on-call path."}],"related":["w17-t01","w17-t03","w17-t07","w17-l1","w11-t07"],"refs":["r16","r23","r5"]},{"id":"w17-t03","order":3,"group":"Oversight","title":"Audit logging and accountability","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An audit log answers who did what, on whose authority, with which evidence and which versions. Log identifiers and decisions by default, keep content on a short, controlled leash, and make the accountability chain explicit.","useIf":"You must reconstruct an agent's action, show a regulator what the system did, or assign accountability after an incident.","avoidIf":"You plan to dump full prompts into a general log store: it copies protected content into a place with weaker access control.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"For high-risk systems, EU AI Act Article 12 requires the ability to record events automatically over the system's lifetime, supporting risk identification, post-market monitoring and monitoring of operation [[r8]]. Deployers must keep the logs under their control for a period appropriate to the purpose, **at least six months**, unless other law, notably data protection law, provides otherwise [[tr1]]. OWASP recommends immutable retrieval logs for RAG [[tr10]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**What to log** is a trade-off between debugging value and exposure. Default to identifiers and decisions: principal, agent identity, tool, argument hashes or ids, retrieved document ids, filter and policy decisions, model, prompt and tool versions, approver and outcome. Capture content only for a bounded window with its own access control, because logs copy ACL-protected content into a store that has no ACLs (design reasoning). **Accountability** needs a chain: the **sponsor** is accountable for purpose and lifecycle, the **owner** administers configuration and credentials, the agent identity acts, and in on-behalf-of mode a delegating user is a second identity to record [[r18]] [[r27]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t03-d1","title":"The accountability chain in one record","alt":"A sponsor and owner govern an agent identity. A delegating user and an approver feed an action. The action produces an immutable audit record.","src":"flowchart LR\n  SP[Sponsor] --> AG[Agent identity]\n  OW[Owner] --> AG\n  US[Delegating user] --> AG\n  AG --> AC[Action]\n  AP[Approver] --> AC\n  AC --> LG[Audit record]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Fields of a useful audit record","head":["Field","Why you need it","Exposure risk"],"rows":[["Principal and agent identity","Who acted and on whose behalf","Low"],["Tool, argument hash or ids, result status","What happened without storing content","Low"],["Retrieved document ids, filter, policy decision","Why the answer contained that data","Low to medium"],["Model, prompt and tool versions","Reproduce behaviour after changes","Low"],["Approver, evidence shown, decision time","Prove human oversight","Medium"],["Prompt and response content","Deep debugging","High: copies protected content"]]}],"whereToUse":["Agent runtimes and tool gateways, at every action","RAG retrieval layers, recording ids, principal and filter hash","Approval systems, recording who saw what evidence","High-risk systems that fall under Article 12 and Article 26","Incident response, where logs decide containment and reporting"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Actions affect people, money or data","A regulator, customer or auditor may ask for evidence","Several identities share responsibility for one action","You need to prove an approval happened"],"avoid":["Logging raw content to a broadly readable store","Logging only successes","Mutable logs that the acting system can edit"],"flips":["Debugging needs content: capture it for a bounded window under its own ACL, then delete","Data-subject deletion applies: store ids and hashes, not personal content","Sampling would drop incidents: keep full audit records and sample only debug traces"]},"alternatives":{"title":"What to put in the log","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Identifiers and decisions only","Audit and compliance, privacy sensitive","Debugging needs the exact prompt","Harder root cause analysis"],["Content with redaction","Debugging with PII masked","Redaction misses secrets","Partial exposure, tooling cost"],["Full content under strict access","Short investigation windows","Long retention or broad readers","Copies protected data, deletion duties"],["No content logging","Highest privacy need","Forensics or regulator evidence","Telemetry gaps in incidents"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A log search returns confidential documents to the wrong people","cause":"Prompts and retrieved text were logged into a store without document-level access control","fix":"Log ids and decisions, restrict content logs to a bounded window and a small group, and set retention"},{"symptom":"Nobody can tell which agent or user caused an action","cause":"Only the service principal was logged, not the delegating user or approver","fix":"Record agent identity, delegating user, sponsor and approver on every action"},{"symptom":"The incident window is missing from the logs","cause":"Head sampling dropped the trace, or retention was too short","fix":"Keep audit records unsampled and retain at least the legal minimum for your class"},{"symptom":"An investigator cannot trust the log","cause":"The acting system can alter its own records","fix":"Write to an append-only or immutable store with separate access"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t03-s1","title":"A high-risk system in the EU","situation":"A deployer runs a system it has classified as high-risk. A regulator may ask what the system did on a given date.","constraints":["Article 12 and 26 apply","Data protection law applies","Records must be tamper-resistant"],"unknowns":["Which logs are under the deployer's control and which the provider keeps?","What retention period does the purpose justify?"],"question":"What do you keep?","options":[{"name":"Automatically generated event logs for at least six months in an immutable store, with ids and decisions, plus a documented retention rule","verdict":"best","why":"It meets the Article 26 floor and supports Article 12 purposes while limiting personal data.","tradeoff":"Storage and governance cost, and some debugging detail is not available."},{"name":"Keep full prompts and outputs forever","verdict":"poor","why":"It collides with data-protection duties and enlarges the breach surface.","tradeoff":"Right only where a rule demands content retention and access is tightly controlled."},{"name":"Keep one month of logs to save cost","verdict":"poor","why":"It is below the six-month minimum unless other law provides otherwise.","tradeoff":"Shorter retention may be right for non-regulated systems."}],"flips":[{"if":"The system is not high-risk","then":"Choose retention by risk and business need."},{"if":"Another law sets a different period","then":"Follow it, and document the reason."}],"takeaway":"Six months is the floor for deployers of high-risk systems."},{"id":"w17-t03-s2","title":"An HR copilot over sensitive documents","situation":"An internal copilot answers questions over permissioned HR documents. Engineers want full prompt logs to debug retrieval.","constraints":["Documents are access-controlled","Staff data is sensitive","Debugging is a real need"],"unknowns":["Who can read the log store?","Can a debug session be time boxed?"],"question":"What do you log?","options":[{"name":"Ids, principal, filter hash and decisions by default; content only for a time-boxed debug window with its own ACL","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps the audit trail and bounds the content copy.","tradeoff":"Some debugging needs a ticket and a window."},{"name":"Log everything to the shared platform log","verdict":"poor","why":"It copies protected HR content into a store with broader readers.","tradeoff":"Fine only for non-sensitive synthetic data."},{"name":"Log nothing about content or retrieval","verdict":"good","why":"Maximum privacy.","tradeoff":"No evidence of what was retrieved, which weakens incident response."}],"flips":[{"if":"Debugging happens on synthetic data","then":"Full logging is safe."},{"if":"A leak is suspected","then":"Extend the content window under approval."}],"takeaway":"Ids by default, content by exception."},{"id":"w17-t03-s3","title":"Many agents, one fleet","situation":"A fleet of agents acts on behalf of users and also autonomously. A customer disputes an action taken yesterday.","constraints":["Mixed on-behalf-of and autonomous modes","Multi-agent hand-offs","Sponsors and owners differ"],"unknowns":["Is there a correlation id across hand-offs?","Does the record include the approver?"],"question":"What must the record show?","options":[{"name":"Agent identity, delegating user if any, sponsor, approver, tool, argument ids, versions and a correlation id across hand-offs","verdict":"best","why":"It lets you attribute the action to people and versions and replay the chain.","tradeoff":"More fields to populate and protect."},{"name":"Service principal and timestamp only","verdict":"poor","why":"Many agents share it, so accountability is lost.","tradeoff":"Enough for a single trivial service."},{"name":"Free-text log lines per agent","verdict":"good","why":"Easy to start.","tradeoff":"Hard to query, join or prove."}],"flips":[{"if":"No human approvers exist","then":"Record the policy decision that allowed the action."},{"if":"Only one agent exists","then":"A simpler record is enough."}],"takeaway":"An unattributed action is an unaccountable action."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t03-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Under Article 26(6), what is the minimum period in months that deployers of high-risk systems keep automatically generated logs under their control, unless other law provides otherwise?","answer":6,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"months","explain":"Article 26(6) says a period appropriate to the intended purpose, of at least six months, unless Union or national law (in particular data protection law) provides otherwise [[tr1]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t03-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which fields belong in a default audit record without copying protected content?","options":["Principal and agent identity","Retrieved document ids and filter decision","Full retrieved passages","Model and prompt versions"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Ids, decisions and versions support audit without copying content. Full passages belong only in a bounded, controlled debug window.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t03-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Why can verbose prompt logging create a data leak?","options":["Logs copy ACL-protected content into a store with weaker access control","Logs slow down the model","Logs change model weights"],"answer":0,"explain":"The risk is access, not performance. Anyone with log access can read content they could not read in the source system.","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do Article 12 and Article 26(6) require?","back":"Art 12: automatic event recording over the lifetime of high-risk systems. Art 26(6): deployers keep logs under their control at least six months unless other law differs."},{"front":"Who is accountable in the Entra Agent ID model?","back":"The sponsor is accountable for purpose and lifecycle, with at least one per agent identity. The owner administers configuration and credentials."},{"front":"Default content policy for audit logs?","back":"Ids and decisions by default. Content only for a bounded window with its own access control."}],"related":["w17-t01","w17-t02","w17-t09","w17-l5","w07-t16","w15-t06"],"refs":["r8","r18","r27","tr1","tr10"]},{"id":"w17-t04","order":4,"group":"Regulation and standards","title":"EU AI Act","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The EU AI Act sorts AI systems by risk and assigns duties to providers and deployers on a dated timetable. The 2026 Omnibus moved the high-risk dates, while transparency and general-purpose AI enforcement began on 2026-08-02.","useIf":"You build or deploy AI for people in the EU and must say which obligations apply to you and from when.","avoidIf":"You treat a blog post date as final or this page as legal advice: confirm legal text with counsel.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The Act is a regulation with **risk tiers**: prohibited practices, **high-risk** systems, systems with **transparency duties**, and **general-purpose AI (GPAI) models**. A system is high-risk under Article 6 when it is a safety component of a product covered by Annex I law that needs third-party conformity assessment, or when it falls in an Annex III use case. Annex III systems can escape the label if they pose no significant risk, such as narrow procedural tasks, but profiling of people is always high-risk, and the provider must document the assessment [[tr5]]. Duties depend on your **role**: providers design and place systems on the market, deployers use them. Examples: Article 14 oversight, Article 12 logging and Article 11 technical documentation for providers [[r5]] [[r8]] [[tr3]]; Article 26 operating duties for deployers [[tr1]]; Article 73 serious incident reporting [[r7]]. Article 4 also asks providers and deployers to support the AI literacy of their staff [[tr2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Penalties under Article 99 reach EUR 35,000,000 or 7% of worldwide turnover for prohibited practices, EUR 15,000,000 or 3% for other operator obligations, and EUR 7,500,000 or 1% for incorrect information. Undertakings face the higher figure, SMEs the lower, and since the Omnibus small mid-caps get the lower figure for the two smaller tiers [[r4]]. The **Digital Omnibus on AI** (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744) was signed on 2026-07-08, published on 2026-07-24 and entered into force on 2026-07-27 (dates from a law-firm alert, secondary-sourced) [[r3]]. It moved Annex III to 2027-12-02 and Annex I to 2028-08-02 [[r2]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Dated obligations (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Date","What applies","Source"],"rows":[["2024-08-01","Entry into force","[[r1]]"],["2025-02-02","Prohibitions and AI literacy","[[r1]]"],["2025-08-02","GPAI obligations; Code of Practice published 2025-07-10, voluntary","[[r1]] [[r9]]"],["2026-08-02","Article 50 transparency applies; enforcement starts for GPAI, prohibitions, transparency and AI literacy","[[r1]]"],["2026-12-02","New prohibitions; Article 50(2) marking transition ends for systems on the market before 2026-08-02","[[r1]] [[r3]]"],["2027-12-02","Annex III high-risk rules","[[r2]]"],["2028-08-02","Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules","[[r2]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Older posts are out of date","md":"Many posts say high-risk rules apply on 2026-08-02. Since the Omnibus entered into force on 2026-07-27 that date is 2027-12-02 for Annex III. Article 50 and GPAI enforcement did start on 2026-08-02. This page is study material, not legal advice."}],"whereToUse":["Scoping an EU launch: which tier, which role, which date","Customer questionnaires that ask for AI Act readiness","Planning logging, documentation and oversight features for 2027","Choosing disclosure and marking features for generative outputs","Vendor reviews of GPAI model providers and their documentation"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the tier and role to decide which duties apply, system by system","Use the dated table to plan the roadmap, not blog posts","Treat Article 50 and AI literacy as live duties as of 2026-08-02"],"avoid":["Claiming certification: the Act is not an ISO-style certificate for chatbots","Treating the GPAI Code of Practice as law","Assuming a GPAI vendor covers your duties as the provider of the system"],"flips":["A use case lands in Annex III: plan for 2027-12-02 and Articles 11, 12, 14 and 26","The system generates synthetic content: Article 50(2) marking applies, with a 2026-12-02 transition for older systems","You fine-tune or rebrand a model: re-check whether you became a provider"]},"alternatives":{"title":"How the Act relates to other frameworks","head":["Framework","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["EU AI Act","EU market access, legal duties","You need a certifiable management system","Binding, with fines and dated duties"],["ISO/IEC 42001","Customers ask for a certificate","You need to show compliance with a specific Article","Certifies an organization's AIMS, not one system"],["NIST AI RMF","A shared risk vocabulary","A regulator wants legal evidence","Voluntary, version 1.0 under revision"],["GPAI Code of Practice","Showing a GPAI provider's Article 53 and 55 approach","You are not a GPAI provider","Voluntary, adequacy-confirmed tool"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A roadmap uses 2026-08-02 for high-risk compliance","cause":"It relies on a pre-Omnibus article or slide","fix":"Use 2027-12-02 for Annex III and 2028-08-02 for Annex I, and cite the Commission page"},{"symptom":"A public chatbot has no AI notice","cause":"Article 50(1) was read as part of the later high-risk wave","fix":"Tell people they are interacting with an AI system at the latest at first interaction, unless obvious"},{"symptom":"A team assumes the model vendor's compliance covers it","cause":"Provider and deployer duties were mixed up","fix":"Map duties per role and per system, and keep vendor documentation on file"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t04-s1","title":"A public chatbot in the EU","situation":"A company runs a customer chatbot for EU users. It is not an Annex III use case. Nobody has added an AI notice.","constraints":["Date of review is 2026-10-13","The chat UI looks like a human agent in places","Engineering capacity is limited"],"unknowns":["Is it obvious to a reasonably informed person that this is an AI?","Does the bot generate synthetic images or audio?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Add a clear notice at first interaction and AI literacy measures for staff","verdict":"best","why":"Article 50 transparency applies since 2026-08-02, and Article 50(5) wants the information clear and distinguishable at the latest at first interaction.","tradeoff":"A UI change and a short training programme."},{"name":"Wait for 2027-12-02, when high-risk rules apply","verdict":"poor","why":"That date concerns Annex III high-risk systems, not Article 50.","tradeoff":"Right for a high-risk system's other duties."},{"name":"Mention AI use in the terms of service only","verdict":"poor","why":"A clause people do not see at first interaction does not meet Article 50(5).","tradeoff":"Fine as an addition to a visible notice."}],"flips":[{"if":"It is obvious to a reasonably well-informed person that they are talking to an AI","then":"Article 50(1) allows an exception, but a notice is cheap insurance."},{"if":"The chatbot makes eligibility decisions about people","then":"Check Annex III and plan for high-risk duties."}],"takeaway":"Article 50 is already live. Do not confuse it with the later high-risk date."},{"id":"w17-t04-s2","title":"Screening candidates in 2027","situation":"A team plans an AI feature in 2027 that ranks job applicants in the EU. They assume they have until 2026-08-02 and are surprised to hear it moved.","constraints":["Likely an Annex III use case","Provider and deployer roles are split across two companies","Launch is mid-2027"],"unknowns":["Does the Article 6(3) derogation apply, given profiling?","Who is the provider?"],"question":"How do you plan?","options":[{"name":"Plan to the 2027-12-02 date, but build logging, oversight and technical documentation into the design now","verdict":"best","why":"Articles 11, 12 and 14 are design-level capabilities that are costly to retrofit, and dates can change again.","tradeoff":"Early engineering cost for duties that apply later."},{"name":"Ignore the Act until late 2027","verdict":"poor","why":"Retrofitting oversight, logs and documentation after launch is slow and risky.","tradeoff":"Reasonable only for systems clearly outside the Act."},{"name":"Exclude EU users","verdict":"good","why":"Avoids the duties.","tradeoff":"Loses the market and may be impractical for hiring tools used by EU employers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Profiling of natural persons is involved","then":"The Article 6(3) derogation does not help: treat it as high-risk."},{"if":"Another Omnibus change moves the date","then":"Re-check the Commission timeline page."}],"takeaway":"Dates moved, but the design work did not."},{"id":"w17-t04-s3","title":"An image generator already on the market","situation":"A product generating images has been on the EU market since before 2026-08-02. The team believes it is exempt from marking rules.","constraints":["As of 2026-10-13","The product outputs synthetic images","Marking is not yet implemented"],"unknowns":["Does the system only assist standard editing?","Which technical marking standard will you use?"],"question":"What is the plan?","options":[{"name":"Implement machine-readable marking and detectability before 2026-12-02, which is 50 days from 2026-10-13","verdict":"best","why":"Systems on the market before 2026-08-02 get until 2026-12-02 for Article 50(2) marking, not an exemption.","tradeoff":"Engineering and testing effort on a fixed deadline."},{"name":"Add a visible watermark only","verdict":"good","why":"Helps humans.","tradeoff":"Article 50(2) asks for machine-readable marking and detectability."},{"name":"Do nothing because the product pre-dates the rule","verdict":"poor","why":"The transition ends on 2026-12-02.","tradeoff":"Right only if the system is out of scope, for example assistive editing that does not substantially alter input."}],"flips":[{"if":"The system only performs an assistive function that does not substantially alter input","then":"The Article 50(2) obligation may not apply."},{"if":"Deployers publish deepfakes","then":"Article 50(4) disclosure duties apply to them."}],"takeaway":"A transition period is a deadline, not an exemption."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"How many days are there from 2026-10-13 to 2026-12-02, the end of the Article 50(2) transition for systems on the market before 2026-08-02?","answer":50,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"days","hint":"18 days left in October, 30 in November, 2 in December","explain":"18 + 30 + 2 = 50 days (derived) [[r3]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t04-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each date to what applies.","pairs":[{"l":"2025-02-02","r":"Prohibitions and AI literacy"},{"l":"2025-08-02","r":"GPAI obligations"},{"l":"2026-08-02","r":"Article 50 transparency and enforcement for GPAI"},{"l":"2027-12-02","r":"Annex III high-risk rules"}],"explain":"These are the Commission timeline dates, with Annex III moved by the Omnibus [[r1]] [[r2]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t04-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"An Annex III system performs profiling of natural persons. Does the Article 6(3) derogation let the provider treat it as not high-risk?","options":["Yes, if the task is narrow","Yes, if human review exists","No, profiling is always high-risk"],"answer":2,"explain":"Article 6(3) lists derogation conditions, but an Annex III system that performs profiling is always considered high-risk [[tr5]].","difficulty":3}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When do Annex III high-risk rules apply after the Omnibus?","back":"2027-12-02. Annex I product-embedded systems follow on 2028-08-02. Article 50 and GPAI enforcement began 2026-08-02."},{"front":"Article 99 maximum fines?","back":"EUR 35M or 7% for prohibited practices, EUR 15M or 3% for other operator duties, EUR 7.5M or 1% for incorrect information. Higher figure for undertakings, lower for SMEs (and small mid-caps on the 3% and 1% tiers)."},{"front":"Is the GPAI Code of Practice law?","back":"No. Published 2025-07-10, it is voluntary, with Transparency, Copyright and Safety and Security chapters."}],"related":["w17-t01","w17-t05","w17-t06","w17-t08","w17-l2","w00-t05"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r7","r8","r9","tr1","tr2","tr3","tr5"]},{"id":"w17-t05","order":5,"group":"Regulation and standards","title":"NIST AI RMF and the GenAI profile","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The NIST AI Risk Management Framework is voluntary guidance with four functions: Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. NIST AI 600-1 adds a Generative AI Profile with twelve risks. It gives a shared vocabulary, not a certificate.","useIf":"A customer, board or team needs a common way to describe AI risk work, especially for generative AI.","avoidIf":"You need a legal compliance statement or a certifiable standard: this is neither.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**AI RMF 1.0** was released on 2023-01-26 and is voluntary. Its four functions are **Govern** (accountability, policy and culture, cross-cutting), **Map** (context, intended use and who is affected), **Measure** (analysing and tracking risk with evidence) and **Manage** (prioritising and acting on risk). NIST states that AI RMF 1.0 is being revised, and on 2026-04-07 it published a concept note for a critical-infrastructure profile, so cite the version you used [[r10]]. The companion **Playbook** offers suggested actions per sub-category. It is neither a checklist nor a set of steps, and NIST says it will be updated after the framework is revised [[tr9]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**NIST AI 600-1**, the Generative AI Profile, was released on 2024-07-26. It names twelve risks: CBRN information or capabilities, confabulation, dangerous, violent or hateful content, data privacy, environmental impacts, harmful bias and homogenization, human-AI configuration, information integrity, information security, intellectual property, obscene, degrading or abusive content, and value chain and component integration. Prompt injection, including indirect injection, and data poisoning sit under information security [[r11]]. Use it as a **risk checklist for threat models and evals**, not as a rulebook."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t05-d1","title":"How the four functions relate","alt":"Govern surrounds the loop. Map feeds Measure, Measure feeds Manage, and Manage feeds back into Map.","src":"flowchart LR\n  G[Govern accountability and policy] -.-> MP\n  G -.-> ME\n  G -.-> MN\n  MP[Map] --> ME[Measure]\n  ME --> MN[Manage]\n  MN --> MP"},{"t":"table","caption":"What each function means for a GenAI application (author synthesis)","head":["Function","Question","Example evidence"],"rows":[["Govern","Who is accountable and what rules apply?","Named sponsors, policy, review cadence, incident roles"],["Map","What is it for, who is affected, what can go wrong?","Use-case record, intended use, applicable 600-1 risks"],["Measure","How do we know?","Groundedness and safety evals, red-team results, monitoring"],["Manage","What do we do about the risk?","Mitigations, approval gates, incident response, retirement"]]}],"whereToUse":["Customer or board questions on 'NIST alignment'","A risk register vocabulary shared by security, legal and engineering","Threat modelling a GenAI app against the twelve 600-1 risks","Structuring an AI governance programme before a certification effort","Mapping evaluation and red-team work to Measure and Manage"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You need a voluntary, widely recognised vocabulary","You want a GenAI risk checklist that includes prompt injection and confabulation","You will map controls to evidence for several audiences"],"avoid":["Claiming NIST compliant or certified: nobody certifies you to it","Using a mapping spreadsheet as proof without records behind each control","Citing 'AI RMF' without a version while it is under revision"],"flips":["A regulator wants legal evidence: map to the EU AI Act duties instead","A customer demands a certificate: pursue ISO/IEC 42001 and borrow NIST vocabulary","NIST publishes the revision: re-map and cite the new version"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing a risk framework","head":["Framework","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["NIST AI RMF and 600-1","Shared vocabulary, GenAI risk checklist","A certificate or legal evidence is needed","Voluntary; version 1.0 under revision"],["ISO/IEC 42001","Certifiable management system","You want a per-risk catalogue for GenAI","Audit cost, org-level scope"],["EU AI Act","Legal duties for the EU market","You need a flexible vocabulary","Binding, dated duties and fines"],["OWASP and MITRE ATLAS","Attack-level threat catalogues","You need governance structure","Technical scope, less organisational"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A deck says 'we are NIST certified'","cause":"A voluntary framework was treated as a certificate","fix":"Say 'aligned with' and show records for each mapped control"},{"symptom":"The mapping looks complete but no evidence exists","cause":"Controls were listed against subcategories without artefacts","fix":"Tie each control to a record: an eval report, an approval log or a review minute"},{"symptom":"The 600-1 list is applied evenly to every system","cause":"No scoping by use case","fix":"In Map, select the risks that apply per system and justify exclusions"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t05-s1","title":"A customer asks if you are NIST aligned","situation":"A US enterprise customer's security questionnaire asks whether your AI product is 'NIST AI RMF compliant'.","constraints":["The framework is voluntary","No certification exists","You have an eval programme and an incident process"],"unknowns":["Which version do they mean?","Do they want evidence or a statement?"],"question":"How do you answer?","options":[{"name":"Say you align your practices to the Govern, Map, Measure and Manage functions and AI 600-1 risks, and attach evidence such as eval reports and incident procedures","verdict":"best","why":"It is accurate and verifiable, and speaks the customer's vocabulary.","tradeoff":"Needs a maintained mapping and evidence pack."},{"name":"Reply 'NIST compliant' to close the deal","verdict":"poor","why":"There is no compliance regime to claim, and an unsupported claim is a risk.","tradeoff":"Not appropriate in any version."},{"name":"Refuse to answer because it is voluntary","verdict":"good","why":"Technically true.","tradeoff":"Loses trust when a short mapping would help."}],"flips":[{"if":"The customer needs a certificate","then":"Offer ISO/IEC 42001 status instead."},{"if":"NIST publishes a revision","then":"Update the mapping and cite the version."}],"takeaway":"Say 'aligned', show records."},{"id":"w17-t05-s2","title":"A five-person team starting governance","situation":"A small team ships a GenAI feature to enterprise users in several regions. They have no AI governance programme and a tight budget.","constraints":["Small team","Multiple jurisdictions","Customers ask about risk management"],"unknowns":["Which jurisdictions bind them?","Do customers require certification?"],"question":"What do you adopt first?","options":[{"name":"Use NIST AI RMF and 600-1 as the working vocabulary and risk checklist, and map EU duties separately where EU users exist","verdict":"best","why":"It is free, voluntary and scalable, and 600-1 gives a concrete GenAI list.","tradeoff":"It does not satisfy legal or certification demands by itself."},{"name":"Start an ISO/IEC 42001 certification programme","verdict":"good","why":"A certificate can unlock enterprise deals.","tradeoff":"Audit cost and effort are heavy for a tiny team before there is a driver."},{"name":"Defer all of it until a customer demands it","verdict":"poor","why":"Risks and records are cheaper to build in than to reconstruct.","tradeoff":"Defer only for prototypes with no real users."}],"flips":[{"if":"A large customer requires certification","then":"Plan 42001, reusing the NIST evidence."},{"if":"The product becomes high-risk under the EU Act","then":"Add Article-level duties."}],"takeaway":"Vocabulary first, certification when a driver appears."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t05-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"How many risks does NIST AI 600-1, the Generative AI Profile, list?","answer":12,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"risks","explain":"AI 600-1 lists twelve GenAI risks, from CBRN information to value chain and component integration. Prompt injection and data poisoning sit under information security [[r11]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t05-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each AI RMF function to its focus.","pairs":[{"l":"Govern","r":"Accountability, policy and culture"},{"l":"Map","r":"Context, intended use and who is affected"},{"l":"Measure","r":"Analysing and tracking risk with evidence"},{"l":"Manage","r":"Prioritising and acting on risk"}],"explain":"Govern is cross-cutting. Map, Measure and Manage form the working loop [[r10]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A customer wants proof of AI governance maturity as a certificate. Which choice fits?","options":["NIST AI RMF, because it certifies organizations","ISO/IEC 42001, borrowing NIST vocabulary","The GPAI Code of Practice"],"answer":1,"explain":"ISO/IEC 42001 is certifiable. NIST AI RMF is voluntary guidance with no certification, and the Code of Practice is a voluntary GPAI compliance tool [[r12]].","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the four AI RMF functions?","back":"Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. Govern is cross-cutting. AI RMF 1.0 was released 2023-01-26 and is voluntary and under revision."},{"front":"What is NIST AI 600-1?","back":"The Generative AI Profile, released 2024-07-26, listing twelve GenAI risks, with prompt injection and data poisoning under information security."},{"front":"Can you be NIST certified?","back":"No. It is voluntary guidance. Say aligned and show evidence for each mapped control."}],"related":["w17-t04","w17-t06","w17-t09","w17-l3","w14-t13"],"refs":["r10","r11","r12","tr9"]},{"id":"w17-t06","order":6,"group":"Regulation and standards","title":"ISO/IEC 42001 and the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"ISO/IEC 42001 is a certifiable management system standard for how an organization governs AI. Microsoft's Responsible AI Standard is an internal rulebook that Microsoft released externally. Neither certifies one chatbot.","useIf":"A customer asks for a certificate, or you are designing an internal AI governance rulebook.","avoidIf":"You want to certify a single application's behaviour or prove EU AI Act compliance with a certificate alone.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**ISO/IEC 42001:2023** is a management system standard for artificial intelligence, an **AIMS**, published in December 2023. It follows Plan-Do-Check-Act and governs an organization's AI practice, not one application's behaviour. It is certifiable: an external audit assesses whether your management system meets the standard [[r12]]. As with other ISO management system standards, expect elements such as a policy, defined roles, risk assessment, operational controls, monitoring, internal audit, management review and improvement (general pattern, not clause by clause)."},{"t":"p","md":"The **Microsoft Responsible AI Standard v2** (June 2022) is an internal standard that Microsoft released externally. It has six goal groups: Accountability, Transparency, Fairness, Reliability and Safety, Privacy and Security, and Inclusiveness. Accountability includes goal A5, human oversight and control, and a **Sensitive Uses review** that is repeated at least annually (A2.3) [[r13]]. It is a rulebook for one company's engineering teams, not a certification you can obtain."},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"How the two fit together","md":"Use ISO/IEC 42001 as the outer assurance wrapper that an auditor can inspect, and a rulebook like the Responsible AI Standard as a model for the inner checkpoints that product teams pass before launch and at each review. One answers 'can you show a working system of management', the other answers 'what must this team do before it ships'."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t06-d1","title":"The Plan-Do-Check-Act loop of an AIMS","alt":"Plan covers scope, policy and risk assessment, Do covers controls and operation, Check covers monitoring and audit, Act covers corrective action, and the loop returns to Plan.","src":"flowchart LR\n  P[Plan scope policy risk] --> D[Do controls and operation]\n  D --> C[Check monitor and audit]\n  C --> A[Act correct and improve]\n  A --> P"},{"t":"table","caption":"Four kinds of document, often confused","head":["Document","Kind","What it gives you","What it does not"],"rows":[["EU AI Act","Law","Legal duties and market access","A management system or a vocabulary"],["NIST AI RMF and 600-1","Voluntary guidance","Shared risk vocabulary and GenAI risk list","A certificate or legal evidence"],["ISO/IEC 42001","Certifiable standard","Third-party assurance of your AIMS","Proof that one system is safe or legal"],["Microsoft Responsible AI Standard","Internal rulebook","A worked example of goals and reviews","A certification you can obtain"]]}],"whereToUse":["Enterprise sales, where customers ask for an AI management certificate","Building an internal AI policy with goals, reviews and owners","Setting an annual Sensitive Uses review for high-impact features","Aligning supplier due diligence across AI vendors","Structuring an internal audit of AI practices"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A buyer or regulator values third-party assurance: pursue ISO/IEC 42001","No external driver yet: adopt an internal rulebook like the RAI Standard's goals and borrow NIST vocabulary","You run many AI systems and need a consistent management process"],"avoid":["Advertising a chatbot as certified because the organization is","Treating a certificate as proof of EU AI Act compliance","Copying a large company's rulebook without scaling it to your size"],"flips":["A major customer demands certification: start the AIMS and reuse existing evidence","Only one product uses AI: a lighter internal rulebook may be enough while that holds","Your risk is regulated high-risk use: add the Act's system-level duties on top"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Which to adopt first","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["ISO/IEC 42001 certification","Buyers want a certificate","No driver, tiny team","Audit cost and ongoing surveillance"],["Internal rulebook (RAI-style)","First programme, no external driver","A buyer demands independent assurance","Self-assessed, uneven rigour"],["NIST AI RMF alignment","Shared vocabulary and risk list","A certificate is required","Voluntary, under revision"],["EU AI Act compliance work","EU market, high-risk systems","No EU exposure","Binding duties and dates"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Marketing says the chatbot is ISO 42001 certified","cause":"Certification of the organization's management system was read as product certification","fix":"Say 'our AI management system is certified' and state the scope; never imply a model or app is certified"},{"symptom":"A certificate exists but nobody follows the process","cause":"The AIMS is a paper exercise without operating evidence","fix":"Run internal audits, track corrective actions and keep records for each control"},{"symptom":"A rulebook is ignored by product teams","cause":"Goals were not translated into reviews and release gates","fix":"Turn goals into checkpoints, for example a Sensitive Uses review before launch and annually after"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t06-s1","title":"An RFP requires an AI management certificate","situation":"A large buyer's RFP requires evidence of an AI management system, ideally a certificate. The supplier has solid engineering practices but no formal system.","constraints":["A multi-year contract depends on it","Practices exist but are undocumented","A year of lead time"],"unknowns":["What scope should the AIMS cover?","Which processes already produce evidence?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Scope an AIMS under ISO/IEC 42001, close gaps, run internal audits and seek certification","verdict":"best","why":"The buyer wants third-party assurance, and a certifiable standard is built for that.","tradeoff":"Audit cost, process overhead and ongoing surveillance."},{"name":"Send the internal rulebook and a NIST mapping","verdict":"good","why":"Fast, shows intent.","tradeoff":"Self-assessed, so it may not satisfy a certificate requirement."},{"name":"Claim the product is certified once the organization is","verdict":"poor","why":"The certificate covers the management system, not the product's behaviour.","tradeoff":"Never appropriate."}],"flips":[{"if":"The buyer accepts a self-attestation","then":"A NIST mapping plus evidence pack may do."},{"if":"The scope is a single product line","then":"Define the AIMS scope narrowly and say so."}],"takeaway":"Certificates assure the system of management, not the chatbot."},{"id":"w17-t06-s2","title":"First internal governance programme","situation":"A mid-size company with several AI features wants consistent rules but has no external certification driver.","constraints":["Several product teams","No regulator demand yet","Leadership wants a visible programme"],"unknowns":["Which products have sensitive uses?","Who owns the rulebook?"],"question":"What do you set up first?","options":[{"name":"An internal rulebook with goals like accountability and human oversight, plus a Sensitive Uses review at launch and annually","verdict":"best","why":"It creates enforceable checkpoints without audit cost, and follows a published model.","tradeoff":"Self-assessed, so credibility with buyers is limited."},{"name":"Pursue ISO certification immediately","verdict":"good","why":"Builds toward assurance.","tradeoff":"Heavy before the processes exist."},{"name":"Leave each team to decide","verdict":"poor","why":"Inconsistent controls and no accountable owner.","tradeoff":"Fine only for prototypes."}],"flips":[{"if":"A buyer requires a certificate","then":"Convert the rulebook into an AIMS."},{"if":"A feature is classified high-risk","then":"Add the Act's system-level duties."}],"takeaway":"Start with enforceable checkpoints. Add assurance when it pays."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t06-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does an ISO/IEC 42001 certificate attest?","options":["A specific chatbot is safe and legal","The organization's AI management system meets the standard","The organization complies with the EU AI Act"],"answer":1,"explain":"42001 is a management system standard. It governs the organization's AI practice, not one application's behaviour, and it is not an EU AI Act compliance certificate [[r12]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t06-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"How many goal groups does the Microsoft Responsible AI Standard v2 define?","answer":6,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"groups","explain":"Accountability, Transparency, Fairness, Reliability and Safety, Privacy and Security, and Inclusiveness [[r13]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t06-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"No customer or regulator requires certification. Which first step fits best?","options":["An ISO audit within a month","No process until an incident","An internal rulebook with launch and annual reviews"],"answer":2,"explain":"A rulebook creates checkpoints cheaply. An immediate audit is heavy without existing processes, and waiting for an incident is reactive.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is ISO/IEC 42001?","back":"A certifiable AI management system standard (AIMS), published December 2023, run as Plan-Do-Check-Act. It governs organizational practice, not one application's behaviour."},{"front":"What are the six goal groups of Microsoft's RAI Standard v2?","back":"Accountability, Transparency, Fairness, Reliability and Safety, Privacy and Security, Inclusiveness. Includes A5 human oversight and an annual Sensitive Uses review."},{"front":"Law, guidance, standard, rulebook?","back":"EU AI Act is law. NIST is voluntary guidance. ISO 42001 is a certifiable standard. The RAI Standard is an internal rulebook."}],"related":["w17-t04","w17-t05","w17-t07","w17-l3","w12-t17"],"refs":["r12","r13","r26"]},{"id":"w17-t07","order":7,"group":"Operating governance","title":"Agent governance lifecycle","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Govern a fleet of agents through five stages: inventory, identity, permissions, review and retirement. Every agent needs a sponsor, an identity with limited access, periodic review and a full shutdown path.","useIf":"Agents are multiplying across teams and nobody can list them, say who owns them or revoke their access quickly.","avoidIf":"You have one agent in a pilot: a simple owner, a ticket and a retirement date may be enough.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Inventory**: an unlisted agent is an ungoverned agent, so register every agent at build or deploy time. **Identity**: give each agent its own identity, not a shared service account. **Permissions**: grant least privilege and review it. **Review**: reconfirm purpose, access and behaviour on a schedule. **Retirement**: remove identity, credentials, memory, data and the registry entry. OWASP's agentic list names the failure modes this guards against, such as ASI03 Identity and Privilege Abuse and ASI10 Rogue Agents [[r22]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Microsoft's control plane for this is **Agent 365**, generally available from 2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, licensed per user, with Microsoft E5 as the best-fit prerequisite. It observes, governs and secures agents through a registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center, Entra, Purview and Defender [[r17]] [[r24]]. In **Entra Agent ID**, at least one **sponsor** is required for each agent identity and blueprint: a business representative accountable for purpose and lifecycle. **Owners** are optional technical administrators. Sponsors can disable or soft-delete; owners can also re-enable, restore and hard-delete [[r18]]. Agents have no MFA, so access is governed with Conditional Access, which needs Microsoft 365 E7 or an Agent 365 licence with at least Entra P1 or Microsoft 365 E3 [[r19]]. An agent identity is a service principal without credentials of its own; the blueprint holds credentials, and execution modes are S2S, OBO and Agentic-User, which needs the Frontier preview program [[r27]]. At the GA announcement, discovery of local and shadow agents through Defender and Intune was preview [[r24]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t07-d1","title":"States of a governed agent","alt":"An agent moves from proposed to registered to active. Active agents are reviewed periodically, can be suspended on an incident or lapsed sponsor, and are eventually retired.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Proposed\n  Proposed --> Registered: sponsor and identity assigned\n  Registered --> Active: permissions approved\n  Active --> InReview: periodic review\n  InReview --> Active: access reconfirmed\n  Active --> Suspended: incident or lapsed sponsor\n  Suspended --> Active: restored\n  Active --> Retired: sponsor retires it\n  Retired --> [*]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Stage, control, accountable role and evidence","head":["Stage","Control","Accountable","Evidence"],"rows":[["Inventory","Registry entry required before deploy; the Agent 365 registry distinguishes Microsoft, partner-built, published-by-your-org and shared-by-creator agents [[r20]]","Platform team","Registry record, discovery reports"],["Identity","Own agent identity, sponsor, optional owner","Sponsor and owner","Identity object, sponsor assignment"],["Permissions","Least privilege, Conditional Access","Owner and security","Access packages, policy config"],["Review","Periodic access and purpose review","Sponsor","Review decisions, logs"],["Retirement","Disable, revoke, delete data, delist","Sponsor","Decommission checklist"]]}],"whereToUse":["A central AI platform that registers agents from many teams","Microsoft 365 estates where Agent 365 and Entra Agent ID apply","Security reviews of agent access and sponsorship","Mergers and reorgs, where sponsors leave and agents are orphaned","Compliance evidence that agents are inventoried and reviewed","Usage visibility and threat signals through Purview Data Security Posture Management for AI [[r14]] and Defender for Cloud AI threat protection [[r15]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["More than a handful of agents exist across teams","Agents hold credentials or act on user data","An auditor or regulator asks who owns each agent","Sponsors change jobs regularly"],"avoid":["Governance by spreadsheet that nobody updates","Shared service accounts for many agents","Granting standing broad access because review is hard"],"flips":["Agents run outside Microsoft 365: require registration at deploy time as a CI gate, and sync to a central inventory","A sponsor leaves: reassign before the agent is suspended","An incident occurs: suspend the identity first, then investigate"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where the inventory lives","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Agent 365 registry and Entra Agent ID","Microsoft 365-centric estate","Many agents run outside it","Licensing prerequisites; some discovery is preview"],["Own registry with a deploy-time gate","Mixed runtimes and clouds","No platform team to run it","You build and maintain it"],["Both, synced","Large mixed estate","Small estate","Duplicated effort, sync drift"],["Spreadsheet","A pilot","Anything with real access","Out of date within weeks"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An agent keeps running after its builder left","cause":"No sponsor succession; the sponsor was a person who moved on","fix":"Require at least one sponsor, prefer a group or two people, and alert on lapsed sponsors [[r18]]"},{"symptom":"Agents share one service account","cause":"Identity was an afterthought","fix":"Give each agent its own identity and sponsor; avoid shared credentials"},{"symptom":"A retired agent still has access","cause":"Only the app was deleted, not the identity, credentials, memory and registry entry","fix":"Use a decommission checklist covering identity, credentials, memory, data and delisting"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t07-s1","title":"Orphaned agents after a reorg","situation":"After a reorganisation, an access review finds eleven agents whose sponsors have left the company. Several still hold read access to HR data.","constraints":["Agents are in Entra Agent ID","Business wants most to keep running","Sponsors are required"],"unknowns":["Which agents still serve a purpose?","Who can be a successor sponsor?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Suspend the orphaned identities, assign successor sponsors per agent, then reinstate those that pass review","verdict":"best","why":"It stops access first, restores accountability and keeps useful agents.","tradeoff":"A short outage for agents that matter."},{"name":"Leave them running until someone complains","verdict":"poor","why":"Agents with data access and no accountable sponsor are a risk with no owner.","tradeoff":"Tolerable only for read-only, non-sensitive agents with a deadline."},{"name":"Delete all eleven","verdict":"good","why":"Clean.","tradeoff":"Loses useful agents and evidence, and owners may be able to restore only soft-deleted ones."}],"flips":[{"if":"An agent is business critical and low-risk","then":"Assign an interim sponsor immediately."},{"if":"Sponsors keep leaving","then":"Use groups of eligible group types as sponsors."}],"takeaway":"Every agent needs a living sponsor."},{"id":"w17-t07-s2","title":"A mixed estate across clouds","situation":"Agents run in Microsoft 365, in a Kubernetes cluster and in another vendor's platform. Leadership wants one answer to 'how many agents do we have and who owns them'.","constraints":["Several runtimes","No single control plane covers all","Audit in six months"],"unknowns":["Which runtimes can integrate with Agent 365 or Entra Agent ID?","Who runs the platform team?"],"question":"How do you inventory?","options":[{"name":"A deploy-time registration gate for every runtime, synced to a central inventory that includes Agent 365 where applicable","verdict":"best","why":"A gate makes registration a condition of running, regardless of runtime, and the central view answers the question.","tradeoff":"You build and maintain the gate and sync."},{"name":"Rely on Agent 365 discovery alone","verdict":"good","why":"Strong inside Microsoft 365.","tradeoff":"Shadow discovery was announced as preview at GA, and other runtimes may not be covered."},{"name":"A shared spreadsheet","verdict":"poor","why":"It is stale on day two and has no enforcement.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a short pilot."}],"flips":[{"if":"Nearly all agents are in Microsoft 365","then":"Agent 365 and Entra become the primary inventory."},{"if":"Discovery reaches GA for your runtimes","then":"Use it to find unregistered agents and close the gap."}],"takeaway":"Make registration a condition of running."},{"id":"w17-t07-s3","title":"Retiring an agent","situation":"A team shuts down a customer-support agent and deletes its repository. Months later its credentials are found to be valid and its memory store still contains customer text.","constraints":["Customer data is involved","Identity and credentials outlived the app","The sponsor wants closure"],"unknowns":["Which stores hold memory and logs?","Which credentials belong to the blueprint?"],"question":"What should retirement have included?","options":[{"name":"A checklist: disable the identity, revoke credentials, delete memory and data, remove permissions and delist from the registry","verdict":"best","why":"Retirement is complete only when identity, credentials, memory, data and the registry entry are gone.","tradeoff":"Effort at the end of a project, and evidence must be retained where law requires."},{"name":"Delete the repository and the deployment","verdict":"poor","why":"The identity, credentials and data survive.","tradeoff":"Enough only for a throwaway prototype with none of those."},{"name":"Disable the identity and leave it for later","verdict":"good","why":"Stops access quickly.","tradeoff":"Credentials and data persist, and later cleanup gets forgotten."}],"flips":[{"if":"A hold or audit requires evidence","then":"Retain logs under the legal rule and delete the rest."},{"if":"The agent may be restored soon","then":"Soft-delete or suspend with a dated review."}],"takeaway":"Retire the identity, the credentials, the memory and the registry entry."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t07-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the governance stages in order for a new agent, from first to last.","items":["Inventory","Identity","Permissions","Review","Retirement"],"explain":"An agent is registered, given its own identity and sponsor, granted least-privilege permissions, reviewed on a schedule and finally retired. This mirrors the five-stage lifecycle used in the Agent 365 and Entra guidance [[r17]] [[r18]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In Entra Agent ID, which statement is correct?","options":["At least one sponsor is required per agent identity; owners are optional","Owners are required and sponsors are optional","Both are optional"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sponsors are required for agent identities and blueprints. Owners are optional technical administrators [[r18]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which belong in an agent decommission checklist?","options":["Disable and delete the agent identity","Revoke credentials held by the blueprint","Delete memory and stored customer data","Delete only the code repository"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Identity, credentials, memory and data must go with the registry entry. Deleting only the repository leaves live access behind.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Five stages of agent governance?","back":"Inventory, identity, permissions, review, retirement. An unlisted agent is an ungoverned agent."},{"front":"Sponsor versus owner in Entra Agent ID?","back":"Sponsor: required business representative accountable for purpose and lifecycle. Owner: optional technical administrator who also manages credentials."},{"front":"When was Agent 365 generally available?","back":"2026-05-01 for the Commercial segment, per user, with Microsoft E5 as best fit. Shadow-agent discovery was announced as preview then."}],"related":["w17-t02","w17-t03","w17-t09","w17-l4","w12-t15","w12-t17","w12-t18"],"refs":["r14","r15","r17","r18","r19","r20","r22","r24","r27"]},{"id":"w17-t08","order":8,"group":"Operating governance","title":"Documentation and transparency","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Transparency has three audiences: builders who consume model documentation, regulators who ask for technical records, and users who must be told they face an AI or AI-made content. Write a versioned system card and design the user disclosure.","useIf":"You ship an AI system and must explain what it is for, how it was evaluated and what users are told.","avoidIf":"You plan one static document for every audience: a regulator file, an engineer's card and a user notice answer different questions.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Model cards** (Mitchell et al.) are short documents that accompany a trained model and report how it performs across conditions; **datasheets for datasets** (Gebru et al.) do the same for data [[tr6]] [[tr7]]. Microsoft's **Transparency Notes** help system owners understand how a technology works, which choices change behaviour, and why to think about the whole system, including people and environment [[tr8]]. As a deployer, read them and carry the relevant limits into your own documentation."},{"t":"p","md":"The EU AI Act sets minimum records. For high-risk systems, **technical documentation** must be drawn up before the system is placed on the market, kept up to date and contain at least the Annex IV elements; SMEs and small mid-caps may use a simplified form [[tr3]]. **GPAI providers** keep technical documentation for the AI Office and authorities (Annex XI), give downstream providers information (Annex XII), maintain a copyright policy and publish a training-content summary. The documentation duties do not apply to open-source models, except those with systemic risk [[tr4]]. **User disclosure** under Article 50: people must be told they are interacting with an AI system unless obvious, synthetic outputs must be machine-readable marked, deployers must disclose deep fakes and AI-generated public-interest text unless human-reviewed under editorial responsibility, and the information must be clear and distinguishable at the latest at first interaction [[r6]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w17-t08-d1","title":"The documentation stack","alt":"A provider's model card or Transparency Note feeds your system card. The system card feeds regulator-facing technical documentation and user-facing disclosure.","src":"flowchart LR\n  M[Model card or Transparency Note] --> S[Your system card]\n  S --> T[Technical documentation]\n  S --> U[User disclosure]\n  T --> R[Regulator]\n  U --> E[End user]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Documents, audiences and triggers","head":["Document","Audience","Contents","Trigger"],"rows":[["Model card or Transparency Note","Builders and deployers","Capabilities, limits, evaluation, intended use","Provider releases a model"],["System card (yours, author synthesis)","Reviewers, auditors, support","Intended and excluded uses, data and permissions, models and versions, eval results with dates, limits, oversight design","Every release"],["Technical documentation (Annex IV)","Authorities, notified bodies","Evidence of compliance for high-risk systems","Before market placement, kept current"],["User disclosure","End users","AI interaction notice, synthetic content labels","First interaction or exposure"]]}],"whereToUse":["Release checklists: update the system card with every model or prompt change","Vendor reviews: collect model cards and Transparency Notes","High-risk systems: maintain technical documentation under Article 11","Public chat or generation features: design the Article 50 disclosure UI","Open-weight publishing: check Article 53 duties before release"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A system affects people or decisions","Customers, auditors or regulators ask what it does and how it was tested","Models or prompts change often and drift must be traceable","Synthetic content or chat reaches the public"],"avoid":["Marketing claims in documentation without eval evidence","A disclosure buried in terms of service","Publishing exploit details of your defences in public documents"],"flips":["The system is high-risk: add Annex IV technical documentation and keep it current","You publish a general-purpose model: add Article 53 documents and the training summary","Users cannot be reached at first interaction: use persistent labels"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Documentation approaches compared","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Versioned system card per release","Most AI products","Docs are not owned","Drift if not part of release gates"],["Wiki page","Early prototypes","Audits or regulators","Stale and unreviewed"],["Auto-generated from eval and config","Many systems, frequent change","Narrative context is needed","Needs tooling; may omit judgement"],["Model card only","You only consume a model","You ship a system with your own data and tools","Misses system-level risks"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The system card contradicts what the product does","cause":"Prompts, tools or models changed and the card did not","fix":"Make the card a release artefact with an owner and a review step"},{"symptom":"Disclosure is missed by users","cause":"The notice is in terms of service rather than at first interaction","fix":"Show a clear notice at first interaction, in an accessible form [[r6]]"},{"symptom":"Claims of accuracy are challenged","cause":"Numbers were quoted without dataset, date or method","fix":"State the eval set, version, date and confidence, and link the report"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t08-s1","title":"An internal agent on a third-party model","situation":"A team deploys an internal agent built on a hosted model. It can search documents and send messages. Nobody has written down what it is for or how it was tested.","constraints":["Internal users only","Not high-risk","Releases happen weekly"],"unknowns":["Who reviews changes?","Which eval reports exist?"],"question":"What documentation do you produce?","options":[{"name":"A short versioned system card: purpose, exclusions, data and permissions, model and prompt versions, eval results, limits and contact, reviewed each release","verdict":"best","why":"It is proportionate, traceable and reuses the provider's Transparency Note for model limits.","tradeoff":"Costs a few hours per release, so automate where possible."},{"name":"Link the provider's Transparency Note and stop","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap.","tradeoff":"It describes the model, not your system's data, tools and approvals."},{"name":"No document: the code is the documentation","verdict":"poor","why":"Reviewers and auditors cannot see intended use, evals or limits.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a throwaway prototype."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent affects hiring or credit decisions","then":"Treat it as potentially high-risk and add Annex IV-style records."},{"if":"Releases become daily","then":"Generate the card from config and eval outputs."}],"takeaway":"Document the system, not only the model."},{"id":"w17-t08-s2","title":"Marketing publishes AI-made video and text","situation":"A marketing team publishes AI-generated video of a person and an AI-written article on a matter of public interest in the EU.","constraints":["Deployer role","Public audience","Some content is lightly edited by an editor"],"unknowns":["Is the video a deep fake?","Does an editor hold editorial responsibility and review the text?"],"question":"What disclosure do you apply?","options":[{"name":"Disclose the video as artificially generated or manipulated, and disclose the article as AI-generated unless it was human-reviewed under editorial responsibility","verdict":"best","why":"Article 50(4) puts these duties on deployers, with the editorial-review exception for text.","tradeoff":"Visible labels may reduce engagement."},{"name":"Disclose nothing because the provider marks outputs","verdict":"poor","why":"Provider marking under Article 50(2) does not replace the deployer's disclosure duty.","tradeoff":"Right only if the content falls outside the deep-fake and public-interest cases."},{"name":"Put a general note in the website footer","verdict":"good","why":"Some transparency.","tradeoff":"It may not be clear and distinguishable at the time of exposure."}],"flips":[{"if":"An editor reviews and holds editorial responsibility for the text","then":"The text disclosure exception applies."},{"if":"The video is evidently artistic or satirical","then":"Disclosure is limited to the existence of generated content, in a way that does not hamper enjoyment."}],"takeaway":"Deployers disclose. Provider marking is a separate duty."},{"id":"w17-t08-s3","title":"Releasing open weights","situation":"A lab plans to release an open-licence general-purpose model with public weights. It asks whether the Article 53 documentation duties apply.","constraints":["Free and open-source licence","Weights and architecture are public","The model is not assessed as systemic-risk"],"unknowns":["Is any systemic-risk threshold met?","Who prepares the training-content summary?"],"question":"What do you plan?","options":[{"name":"Publish the copyright policy and the training-content summary, and confirm the exemption scope and systemic-risk status with counsel","verdict":"best","why":"Article 53(2) exempts points (a) and (b) for qualifying open-source models, but copyright policy and the training summary remain, and the exemption fails for systemic-risk models.","tradeoff":"Legal review and a public summary of training content."},{"name":"Assume the open licence exempts everything","verdict":"poor","why":"Only the technical documentation and downstream information duties are exempt.","tradeoff":"Right for none of the remaining duties."},{"name":"Prepare full Annex XI and XII documentation anyway","verdict":"good","why":"Safe and useful to downstream users.","tradeoff":"More work than the exemption requires."}],"flips":[{"if":"The model is classed as systemic-risk","then":"No exemption: prepare all documentation and follow Article 55."},{"if":"The licence or weights are not fully open","then":"The exemption may not apply."}],"takeaway":"Read the exemption text. It is narrower than 'open source'."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"By when must Article 50 transparency information be provided to the people concerned?","options":["Within 30 days of first use","Only on request","At the latest at the time of first interaction or exposure"],"answer":2,"explain":"Article 50(5) requires the information to be clear and distinguishable at the latest at the time of the first interaction or exposure, and to meet accessibility requirements [[r6]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which belong in a useful system card?","options":["Intended and excluded uses","Model and prompt versions","Eval results with dates","Marketing claims without evidence"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Purpose, versions and dated eval results make a card traceable. Unsupported claims create liability.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t08-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A provider releases an open-licence general-purpose model with systemic risk. What happens to the Article 53(2) exemption?","options":["It applies fully","It does not apply","It applies only to the copyright policy"],"answer":1,"explain":"The exemption from technical documentation and downstream information duties does not apply to general-purpose models with systemic risk [[tr4]].","difficulty":3}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does a model card do?","back":"A short document accompanying a model that reports performance across conditions (Mitchell et al.). Datasheets for datasets do the same for data (Gebru et al.)."},{"front":"Where must Article 50 information appear?","back":"Clear and distinguishable, at the latest at the first interaction or exposure, and accessible. A terms-of-service clause is not enough."},{"front":"What do GPAI providers document under Article 53?","back":"Technical documentation (Annex XI), downstream provider information (Annex XII), a copyright policy and a public training-content summary. Open-source models are exempt from the first two unless systemic-risk."}],"related":["w17-t04","w17-t05","w17-t09","w17-l5","w14-t01"],"refs":["r6","tr3","tr4","tr6","tr7","tr8"]},{"id":"w17-t09","order":9,"group":"Operating governance","title":"AI incident response","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"AI incidents are probabilistic, context-dependent and hard to root-cause. Extend classic incident response with AI harm categories, domain-weighted severity, AI-specific telemetry, staged remediation and a reporting plan, and rehearse it before you need it.","useIf":"An AI system or agent can cause harm, leak data or act wrongly, and you must contain it and, for some systems, report it.","avoidIf":"You assume your classic security playbook covers it: taxonomy, severity, root cause and telemetry all differ.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Microsoft's guidance names four reasons AI incidents are different. **Taxonomy gaps**: harms such as dangerous instructions or targeted content do not fit confidentiality, integrity and availability buckets. **Context-dependent severity**: wrong medical advice and wrong trivia are not the same incident. **Ambiguous root cause**: behaviour emerges from training data, fine-tuning, retrieval inputs and user context. **Telemetry gaps**: standard monitoring does not see output anomalies or classifier confidence shifts, and privacy-by-design defaults narrow the forensic record [[r21]]. A gap in a safety classifier can produce thousands of harmful outputs before a reviewer sees the first."},{"t":"p","md":"The prescribed **staged remediation** has three stages. **Stage 1**, the first hour: contain by blocking known-bad inputs, activating content filters and restricting access. **Stage 2**, the first 24 hours: expand mitigations to related variants using automated pattern analysis. **Stage 3**, days to weeks: fix at source with classifier updates, model adjustments and systemic changes. Allow and block lists are tactical, not durable, and each stage needs a **watch period**, because non-deterministic behaviour cannot be verified by one test pass [[r21]]. For **high-risk systems**, providers report serious incidents within 15 days of awareness, within 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident, and within 10 days after a death, and an incomplete initial report is allowed. The provider must not alter the system in a way that affects later evaluation of causes before informing the authorities [[r7]]. Deployers who identify a serious incident inform the provider first, then the importer or distributor and authorities, and suspend use if the system presents a risk [[tr1]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Staged remediation","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Detect","md":"Output anomalies, classifier shifts, report spikes, Defender alerts [[r15]].","tag":"signal"},{"title":"Contain","md":"First hour. Disable the identity or tool, block bad inputs, tighten filters, switch to read-only.","tag":"1 hour"},{"title":"Expand","md":"First 24 hours. Cover related variants, roll back the prompt or model.","tag":"24 hours"},{"title":"Fix at source","md":"Days to weeks. Classifier, model and design changes. Keep watch periods.","tag":"days"},{"title":"Learn","md":"Update taxonomy, tests, runbook and records.","tag":"after"}]}],"whereToUse":["Runbooks for assistants and agents with external effects","On-call rotations that include AI quality and safety signals","Regulated deployments with serious-incident reporting duties","Tabletop exercises with security, legal, communications and support","Post-incident reviews that feed evals and guardrails"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Any production AI system with user-facing or action-taking behaviour","Before launch: define roles, kill switches and reporting paths","After each incident: convert findings into tests and monitors"],"avoid":["Treating a blocklist entry as the fix","Declaring done after one successful test","Altering a high-risk system before the authorities have been informed where Article 73(6) applies"],"flips":["A death or widespread infringement is involved: the 10-day or 2-day clocks apply","The incident is an agent action: suspend its identity first, then investigate","Logs hold no content: rely on ids, versions and decisions, and plan bounded content capture next time"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Incident handling approaches compared","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Extend the security playbook with AI categories and stages","You have an existing security IR team","No one owns AI behaviour","Needs new taxonomy and telemetry"],["Separate AI safety response team","Many high-impact systems","Small organisation","Coordination overhead"],["Ad hoc response","A prototype","Any production use","Slow containment, inconsistent severity"],["Vendor-led response only","Provider's model is at fault","The issue is your prompt, data or tools","Limited visibility and control"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The system was rebuilt and the root cause cannot be shown","cause":"The team altered or deleted the system before preserving evidence or informing authorities","fix":"Snapshot logs, versions and configuration first; follow Article 73(6) for high-risk systems [[r7]]"},{"symptom":"Containment took a day","cause":"No kill switch or the identity could not be disabled quickly","fix":"Build per-tool and per-identity disable switches and test them"},{"symptom":"The same harm returns after a fix","cause":"A blocklist entry was treated as durable and no watch period followed","fix":"Use allow and block lists as triage, fix at source, and watch after each stage [[r21]]"},{"symptom":"Responders burn out after a safety incident","cause":"Exposure to harmful content with no rotation or support","fix":"Plan rotation, breaks and peer support in advance; responders meet harmful content in ways malware analysts do not [[r21]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w17-t09-s1","title":"An agent emails the wrong recipients","situation":"A support agent was steered by a poisoned document and sent account summaries to external addresses. The pattern is spotted after 40 minutes.","constraints":["Personal data is involved","The agent has its own identity","Logs hold ids and decisions but not content"],"unknowns":["How many emails left?","Can the sponsor disable the identity immediately?","Which other agents read the same document?"],"question":"What do you do in the first hour?","options":[{"name":"Disable the agent's identity or send tool, quarantine the document, preserve logs and versions, notify the owners","verdict":"best","why":"It stops the leak, removes the vector and keeps evidence, then expands to related agents.","tradeoff":"The agent is offline until review."},{"name":"Patch the prompt and keep the agent running","verdict":"poor","why":"A prompt edit is probabilistic and the poisoned document remains.","tradeoff":"Right for a stage-3 fix, after containment."},{"name":"Wait for the full root cause before acting","verdict":"poor","why":"Containment comes before investigation, and the leak continues meanwhile.","tradeoff":"Waiting is right only when containment would destroy evidence you cannot capture."}],"flips":[{"if":"Several agents read the same corpus","then":"In stage 2, quarantine it for all of them."},{"if":"Personal data left the organisation","then":"Start the notification process with legal and the data protection officer."}],"takeaway":"Contain first, then investigate."},{"id":"w17-t09-s2","title":"Harmful outputs after a model update","situation":"After a provider model update, user reports of harmful replies triple within a day. No code changed on your side.","constraints":["Consumer-facing assistant","Classifier confidence shifted","Providers may take days to respond"],"unknowns":["Can you pin or roll back the model version?","Which prompts trigger it?"],"question":"How do you respond?","options":[{"name":"Roll back or pin the previous version, tighten filters, then expand to related patterns and watch before closing","verdict":"best","why":"Containment is quick and reversible, and the staged plan covers variants and verification.","tradeoff":"You may lose features of the new model until fixed."},{"name":"Add a blocklist of offending prompts","verdict":"good","why":"Fast triage.","tradeoff":"Tactical only: variants slip through."},{"name":"Wait for the provider to fix it","verdict":"poor","why":"Your users are exposed, and the issue may involve your prompt and context.","tradeoff":"Right only if you cannot influence the model or can switch off the feature."}],"flips":[{"if":"No pinning is possible","then":"Switch to a fallback model or disable the feature."},{"if":"Reports spike in one language or group","then":"Weight severity by affected population."}],"takeaway":"Roll back to a known-good version, then fix at source."},{"id":"w17-t09-s3","title":"A suspected serious incident in a high-risk system","situation":"A deployer of a high-risk system learns that a malfunction may have contributed to a person's death. The provider is reachable.","constraints":["EU high-risk system","Possible death","Engineers want to restart and patch immediately"],"unknowns":["When did the provider or deployer become aware?","Is a causal link suspected?"],"question":"What are the first steps?","options":[{"name":"Inform the provider immediately, suspend use if a risk exists, preserve the system state, and let the provider report within the 10-day limit","verdict":"best","why":"Deployers inform the provider first and suspend use on risk, and a death sets a 10-day reporting limit. Preserving state avoids altering evidence before authorities are informed.","tradeoff":"Downtime and a slower fix."},{"name":"Patch and restart first, report later","verdict":"poor","why":"Altering the system before informing authorities can affect evaluation of causes.","tradeoff":"Right only when a safety hazard requires an immediate stop and evidence is captured."},{"name":"Wait 15 days for the standard report window","verdict":"poor","why":"A death shortens the limit to 10 days, and reports should be immediate once a causal link is suspected.","tradeoff":"15 days is the general limit for other serious incidents."}],"flips":[{"if":"The provider cannot be reached","then":"Article 73 applies to the deployer as if it were the provider."},{"if":"It is a widespread infringement","then":"The two-day limit applies."}],"takeaway":"Know the clock before you need it: 15, 2 or 10 days."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w17-t09-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the staged remediation in order.","items":["Immediate containment in the first hour","Expand and strengthen in the first 24 hours","Fix at source over days or weeks","Watch period and closure"],"explain":"Microsoft's guidance prescribes three stages with watch periods after each, because one test pass cannot verify non-deterministic behaviour [[r21]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w17-t09-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Article 73 situation to the reporting limit.","pairs":[{"l":"Serious incident, standard","r":"No later than 15 days"},{"l":"Widespread infringement","r":"No later than 2 days"},{"l":"Death of a person","r":"No later than 10 days"}],"explain":"Article 73 sets 15 days after awareness as the general limit, 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident, and 10 days for a death. An incomplete initial report is allowed [[r7]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w17-t09-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A blocklist stopped the harmful outputs, then similar ones returned a week later. What was missed?","options":["A bigger blocklist is always sufficient","Fixing at source and a watch period after each stage","Disabling logging"],"answer":1,"explain":"Allow and block lists are tactical triage. Stage 3 fixes the classifier, model or design, and watch periods verify the fix [[r21]]. A bigger list leaves variants, and removing logs hurts.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why do AI incidents differ from classic security incidents?","back":"Taxonomy gaps, context-dependent severity, ambiguous root cause and telemetry gaps. Outputs are probabilistic and one gap can cause thousands of harms."},{"front":"What are the three remediation stages?","back":"Contain in the first hour, expand in the first 24 hours, fix at source over days or weeks, with a watch period after each."},{"front":"What are the Article 73 reporting limits?","back":"15 days after awareness, 2 days for widespread infringement or Article 3(49)(b), 10 days after a death. An incomplete initial report is allowed."}],"related":["w17-t03","w17-t04","w17-t07","w17-l5","w16-t10","w14-t13"],"refs":["r7","r15","r21","tr1"]}]});
